% \section{Introduction}
%
% This package introduces some environments that are useful for writing 
% teaching material.
% It also provides a few settings for |beamer| and |memoir| to make the 
% resulting document more readable.
%
% \section{Usage}
%
% The package automatically detects if |beamer| or |memoir| is loaded and 
% customizes the document accordingly.
% \DescribeOpt{nobeamer}
% \DescribeOpt{noarticle}
% \DescribeOpt{nomemoir}
% That can be prevented, however, by using the |nobeamer| or |nomemoir| 
% options.
% The |noarticle| is to prevent the article versions of the (|beamer|) 
% environments to be defined.
%
% \DescribeOpt{notheorems}
% The package also provides a few environments for |beamer| and |article| mode.
% Particularly, it can define theorems and definitions, which might be defined 
% also by other packages.
% To prevent this, use the |notheorems| option.
%
% \DescribeOpt{inner}
% \DescribeOpt{outer}
% \DescribeOpt{top}
% \DescribeOpt{bottom}
% \DescribeOpt{marginparmargin}
% When the package customizes |memoir| it sets the inner and outer margins to 
% fit margin notes better.
% The |inner| and |outer| options can be used to set the inner and outer 
% margins to be used.
% The |top| and |bottom| options can be used to set the top and bottom margins.
%
% The |marginparmargin| is passed on to the |memoir| class and sets which 
% margin to use for the margin notes, and for the captions of |memoir|'s 
% |sidecaption|.
%
% \DescribeOpt{sidecapalign}
% A figure or table with a |sidecaption| has its caption in the margin, so 
% its contents should go to the side of the caption, or a narrow table 
% leaves a gap between itself and its caption.
% That is the default, |sidecapalign=caption|: the contents go to the right 
% of the text block when the caption is in the right margin, and to the left 
% when it is in the left margin, also with |outer| or |inner| margins in a 
% two-sided document.
% Contents as wide as the text, such as |\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]|, 
% don't move.
% The contents are made as narrow as they can be, so stretchable space 
% between them is lost: two images with |\hfill| between them end up side 
% by side at the caption.
% To spread them over the text width, put them in 
% |\makebox[\linewidth]{...}|.
% Footnotes in the contents can make the \textsf{varwidth} package give up 
% (it warns that it ``failed to reprocess'' them), and then the contents 
% stay where |memoir| puts them; in our tests that happened with two 
% |\footnote|s, not with one or with \textsf{tablefootnote}.
% With |sidecapalign=off|, the contents are placed as in |memoir|: at the 
% left, unless the float centres them.
% \DescribeMacro{\flushscap}
% Then |\flushscap| at the start of the |sidecaption| flushes those 
% contents towards the caption, one float at a time.
%
% \DescribeOpt{footnotereuse}
% With \textsf{biblatex}, citations become footnotes, and a citation of a 
% source that already has a footnote on the same page (or spread, with 
% |twoside|) reuses that footnote's number instead of repeating it.
% Since the page is only known from the previous pass, this needs a few 
% passes to settle, and a citation whose footnote would move it to another 
% page reserves the space of its footnote, leaving a blank in the margin.
% The |footnotereuse| option chooses how:
% \begin{description}
% \item[compact] Reserve the space only when a citation has changed its mind 
%   back again. This leaves the fewest blanks. This is the default.
% \item[fast] Reserve the space as soon as a citation changes its mind 
%   between passes and reuses a footnote. This needs a pass less, but when 
%   many pages move at once, for instance when the |.aux| file is deleted 
%   and the bibliography isn't, it leaves blanks that stay until those 
%   citations change their minds again.
% \item[off] Give every citation its own footnote. This needs no extra 
%   passes. It's the default with the |draft| option, of the class or the 
%   package, to compile faster; |footnotereuse=compact| overrides that.
% \end{description}
% Either way, a mark only refers to a footnote on its own page, or to one 
% that the margin moved on from a mark on the same page (its own footnote 
% would have moved too). If a citation can't settle, it gets its own footnote, 
% and a warning tells you where that repeats one.
% In a long document, the first compilation may need more passes than 
% |latexmk| allows by default (5); it continues from where it stopped the next 
% time, or you can raise |$max_repeat|.
%
% \subsection{Beamer blocks and semantic environments for papers}
%
% \DescribeEnv{assumption}
% \DescribeEnv{idea}
% \DescribeEnv{question}
% \DescribeEnv{exercise}
% \DescribeEnv{activity}
% \DescribeEnv{remark}
% These environments provides wrappers around Beamer's |block| environment.
% The idea is to complement the blocks provided by Beamer, such as |theorem|, 
% |definition| and |example|, with some more useful blocks.
% Each of them provides a block with an appropriate title and optional subtitle 
% and is of a suitable colour.
% For instance, consider the following.
% \input{idea.tex}
% That can be produced by the following code.
% \inputminted[linenos]{latex}{idea.tex}
%
% \DescribeMacro{\ProvideSemanticEnv}
% We can create new such block environments, that work both with |beamer| and
% without, by using the |\ProvideSemanticEnv| command.
% This command takes five arguments:
% \begin{enumerate}
% \item the name of the environment,
% \item the name of the block environment to use (optional),
% \item the title of the block,
% \item options to pass to |thmtools| (optional),
% \item the key of the translation of the refname of the block,
% \item the key of the translation of the refnames of the block.
% \item the key of the translation of the Refname of the block,
% \item the key of the translation of the Refnames of the block.
% \end{enumerate}
% For example, the |idea| block above was created by the following code:
% \inputminted{latex}{ProvideSemanticEnv.tex}
%
% \DescribeEnv{lightblock}
% \DescribeEnv{darkblock}
% We provide two shades of boxes, light coloured and dark coloured boxes.
% These can be used to create boxes of various colours easily.
% These two environments take two mandatory arguments: the first one is a 
% colour, the second is the title.
%
% In the text (not slides) the blocks are not coloured.
% For instance, consider the following.
% \input{lightblock.tex}
% That can be produced by the following code.
% \inputminted[linenos]{latex}{lightblock.tex}
%
% \DescribeEnv{blackblock}
% \DescribeEnv{whiteblock}
% \DescribeEnv{bwblock}
% For instance, we provide a black block, white block and a black-white block.
% These take only one mandatory argument: a title.
%
% \DescribeEnv{redblock}
% \DescribeEnv{blueblock}
% \DescribeEnv{purpleblock}
% \DescribeEnv{greenblock}
% \DescribeEnv{yellowblock}
% \DescribeEnv{orangeblock}
% We also provide a few predefined blocks in various common colours.
% They all take a title as mandatory argument.
%
% \subsection{Frame titles in the notes}
%
% In the slides a frame title stands on a line of its own, at the top of the
% frame.
% In the notes (|beamerarticle|), |beamer| makes it a heading followed by an
% empty line, so the frame's text starts a new paragraph under it, which
% reads as if the frame had two paragraphs where it has one.
% We make the frame title a run-in heading instead, like |\paragraph|: the
% title in bold, and the frame's text after it on the same line.
% A subtitle follows the title in the heading, after a dash and in italics.
% A frame that starts with a list, a block, a display or code gets the title on a
% line of its own above it, as after any run-in heading.
% So does a frame that starts with a picture (|\includegraphics|, |tikzpicture|),
% a table (|tabular|, |tabular*|, |tabularx|), a |minipage|, |columns| or
% |\tableofcontents|, which would otherwise stand beside the title; and a frame
% that starts with a figure or table float, which could otherwise come above the
% title: then the text after the float doesn't run in with the title either.
% Any other box (an |\fbox|, a |\parbox|, a |\rule|) is set in the line like a
% word, as after any run-in heading, so it stands beside the title.
% To put one under the title, write it in a |center|.
% The slides are unchanged.
% The titles of |beamer|'s blocks (|block|, |alertblock|, |exampleblock|) 
% are run-in headings in the notes in the same way, in the fonts that 
% |beamer| gives them there: bold, bold italic and italic.
% The option |noarticle| keeps |beamerarticle|'s frame and block titles.
% A template |frametitle| of your own in the preamble wins too, wherever it
% stands, before or after the packages.
% If it's |beamerarticle|'s default you want, use |noarticle|: writing
% |\setbeamertemplate{frametitle}[default]| only works after both packages, since
% before |didactic| it can't be told from not having set a template.
%
% \subsection{Adding margin notes of different kinds}
%
% \DescribeMacro{\indentedmarginpar}
% We want to be able to put quite some content in the margins.
% For this reason we want to have indented paragraphs in the margin notes.
% We can use the |\indentedmarginpar{text}| command to do this.
% For instance, consider the output in the margin.
% \indentedmarginpar{%
%   This is an example of an indented margin paragraph.
%
%   It can contain multiple paragraphs.
% }
%
% \DescribeMacro{\newnotetype}
% \DescribeMacro{\ltnote}
% We also want to be able to use titled notes of different kinds.
% We can use the |\newnotetype[fmtcmd]{type}{title}| command to define a new 
% type of note.
% For instance |\ltnote| is defined as
% \begin{center}
% |\newnotetype{\ltnote}{\GetTranslationWarn{Learning theory}}|.
% \end{center}
% \ltnote{This is an example of a learning theory note.}
%
% We can also change the formatting of the title.
% |\newnotetype[\textbf]{\bfnote}{Bold note}| and
% |\bfnote{This is a bold note.}| gives us the note in the margin.
% \newnotetype[\textbf]{\bfnote}{Bold note}
% \bfnote{This is a bold note.}
% \ltnote{Another example of a learning theory note.}
%
% If the \textsf{corrigenda} package is loaded, its margin notes (the 
% reviewers' comments on errata) are also typeset with |\indentedmarginpar|, 
% so that they look like the other notes.
%
% \DescribeMacro{\NewNoteType}
% We also have |\NewNoteType| which is almost synonymous to |\newnotetype|.
% The difference is that |\NewNoteType| doesn't take a command as argument, but 
% just a name:
% |\NewNoteType{ltnote}{Learning theory}| instead.
% Then we also get |\ltnoteoff| to turn off the note and |\ltnoteon| to turn it 
% back on.
%
% Notes are off by default in |beamer|, but on by default otherwise.
%
% These notes are commentary, so they may give way to footnotes, which must 
% stay with their marks.
% When the margin is full, \textsf{marginfix} places the notes in the order of 
% their anchors, so a footnote after a long note may be moved to the next 
% page, away from its mark.
% \DescribeOpt{notepriority}
% From version~1.5, \textsf{marginfix} lets notes marked as deferrable give 
% way, and we mark all the notes above that way.
% The option |notepriority| chooses how (it sets \textsf{marginfix}'s 
% |\marginnotepriority|):
% \begin{description}
% \item[split] A note that crowds out a footnote is split at the end of the 
%   page and continues on the next, and a note that doesn't fit no longer 
%   holds back the footnotes after it. This is the default.
% \item[defer] Such a note moves to the next page whole.
% \item[warn] Nothing moves, but a warning names the notes that crowded out 
%   a footnote, so that you can shorten or move them. This is 
%   \textsf{marginfix}'s own default.
% \item[off] Like |warn|, without the warnings.
% \end{description}
% With an older \textsf{marginfix}, none of this happens, and the notes are 
% placed as before.
%
% \textsf{marginfix} splits a note at a paragraph break if there is one near 
% the end of the page, and starts the rest with a cue, which we set to 
% ``(continued)'' in the language of the document, ``(forts.)'' in Swedish.
% The words are the translations |continued| and |continued on the next page| 
% of the \textsf{translations} package, so other things that continue on the 
% next page can use the same words, for instance the head and foot of a long 
% table:
% |\emph{\tablename~\thetable{} (\GetTranslation{continued})}| and
% |\emph{\GetTranslation{continued on the next page}}|.
%
% \DescribeOpt{notebesidefloats}
% \textsf{marginfix} normally keeps the margin beside a float at the bottom 
% of the page empty, in case the float reaches into the margin; so notes 
% after it move to the next page.
% That is the default, |notebesidefloats=reserve|.
% With |notebesidefloats=use|, notes go beside a bottom float, unless the 
% float reaches into the margin in a way \textsf{marginfix} sees: it is wider 
% than the text or has lines that stick out of it (our |fullwidth|, or a 
% table that is too wide), it has a side caption, or it contains 
% |\marginnotereservefloat|.
% Use that command in a float that reaches into the margin in a way that 
% \textsf{marginfix} can't see: content that sticks out of a |minipage|, 
% |\parbox| or |tabular| centred on its line (the default; at the top or 
% bottom of the line it is seen), of an |\fbox| or a formula, a picture drawn 
% beyond its box, or text set with |\rlap|.
% Such a float would get notes set over it without a warning, which is why 
% |use| isn't the default; choose it when you know your bottom floats, or 
% mark the ones that reach out.
% Floats at the top of the page always keep the margin empty.
%
% \DescribeOpt{noteoverflow}
% Even so, the notes of a page can be more than its margin holds, say in a 
% paragraph dense with citations, whose footnotes are in the margin too.
% Then a note moves to the next page, away from its mark.
% With |noteoverflow=break|, the default, the text gives way instead: the 
% page ends before the line that calls out the first note that doesn't fit, 
% in the next run, so that line and its note start the next page.
% That can move more text and more notes, so a page dense with notes can take 
% several runs, up to about ten; \LaTeX\ asks for a rerun until they are done.
% A page that ends that early is a little shorter, but its margin is full; 
% if its text fills less than half of it, \textsf{marginfix} warns, naming 
% the page.
% With |noteoverflow=carry| the notes move to the next page as before, which 
% is the default with the option |draft|.
%
% This needs \textsf{marginfix} 1.6 or later.
% With an older one the option, and |\marginnoteoverflow| below, do nothing and 
% say so in a warning.
% It also needs pdf\LaTeX\ or Lua\LaTeX: with Xe\LaTeX\ the notes are carried, and 
% a warning names the pages of each.
% The mode is read where a note is called out, and obeys groups, so 
% |{\marginnoteoverflow{carry}| ...|}| lets the notes of one passage move to 
% the next page, while the rest of the document keeps |break|.
% \textsf{marginfix} logs every note that is still carried to a later page, 
% as |marginfix-spill: page=|\meta{p}| to=|\meta{q}| intended| for a note in 
% the mode |carry| and |... unresolved| otherwise.
%
% Outside |beamer|, \textsf{didactic} writes where the margin column is to 
% the log, once, as |didactic-margin: odd=431.65 even=431.65 width=156.7|: 
% the left edge of the margin column on odd and even pages and its width, 
% in big points (\texttt{bp}) from the left edge of the page, as in the 
% PDF.
% A script that checks that each margin note is printed on the page of its 
% mark can read it there instead of guessing it.
% The line assumes one column of text: with |twocolumn|, where the notes of 
% each column go into the margin beside it, it doesn't say where they are.
%
% \subsection{Figures and tables in the margin}
%
% \DescribeEnv{marginfigure}
% \DescribeEnv{margintable}
% A small figure or table can go in the margin of the notes, beside the text
% that discusses it, with its caption under it.
% |memoir| has the environments |marginfigure| and |margintable| for this,
% and we make them work in a source shared with the slides:
% \begin{description}
% \item[In the notes] (|memoir|) the float is a |\marginpar|, so
%   \textsf{marginfix} places it with the margin notes, in the order in which
%   they stand in the text.
%   It is set ragged, like the notes, and its caption is numbered with the
%   other figures or tables, so |\cref| refers to it as to any figure or
%   table.
% \item[On the slides] (|beamer|) it is an ordinary |figure| or |table|,
%   centred, with its caption.
% \item[Elsewhere] (another class, without |beamer|) it is an ordinary
%   float.
% \end{description}
% For instance, in the notes the following frame puts the picture in the
% margin, beside the sentence, and on the slide it puts the picture under
% the sentence.
% \inputminted[linenos]{latex}{marginfloats.tex}
% In the notes |\linewidth| is the width of the margin, and on the slides 
% it's the width of the frame, so a picture needs a height too, as here, 
% or it fills the slide.
%
% Write the environment where the float belongs: before the paragraph that
% discusses it, as above, or inside the paragraph, where you would write a
% |\marginpar|.
% Before the paragraph, the top of the float is level with the paragraph's
% first line; inside it, the float hangs from the line where it stands.
% Like |\marginpar|, the environment adds no space to the text: a space
% before it and one after it leave one space between the words around it.
% In a frame that is only on the slides (|\begin{frame}<presentation>|), the
% float has no effect on the notes: it isn't set and takes no number, and a
% |\label| in it is undefined there.
%
% We change two things in |memoir|'s environments.
% |memoir| ends the paragraph at a margin float and, whenever an ordinary
% float is still waiting to be placed, starts a new page
% (|\FloatBlock|), to keep the figures in the order of their numbers.
% In notes with many figures, that leaves pages half empty.
%
% \DescribeOpt{marginfloatorder}
% The option |marginfloatorder| says which of the two you get:
% \begin{description}
% \item[beside] (the default) The margin float stays beside its text: the
%   paragraph goes on and no page ends.
%   A figure that is still waiting may then come on a later page with a lower
%   number, so the numbers can come out of page order.
%   The warning described below says when they did.
% \item[inorder] The numbers stay in page order, as in |memoir|: the
%   environment ends the paragraph, and starts a new page when an ordinary
%   float is waiting, also one for the bottom of the current page.
%   The price is that half-empty page.
% \end{description}
% To keep the order for one float, write |\FloatBlock| before the environment
% with |beside|.
%
% Whatever the option, at the end of each run we compare the pages where the
% captions ended up, for all floats of each kind (figures, tables, a float type
% of your own) and not only for margin floats.
% A caption on a later page than one numbered after it gives one warning, which
% names both numbers, both pages and both input lines; numbers on the same page
% don't count.
% That also covers a margin float that \textsf{marginfix} moves on to the next
% page, and a figure with |[H]| that overtakes one that waits.
% The warning takes no extra run, since it reads what the same run wrote; it does
% mean that it can appear in a run where the pages aren't final yet, so only
% believe the one from the last run.
% We see a caption when it goes into its list (|\addcontentsline|), so a caption
% that doesn't (|\captionsetup{list=no}| from the package \textsf{caption}) isn't
% compared, and neither is anything under |\nofiles|, which writes no \texttt{.aux}.
% It stays on in a draft, as it costs next to nothing.
% On the slides (the class |beamer|) floats don't move, so there is nothing to
% check.
%
% |memoir| also raises a margin float by 1.2\,ex, which pushes it into the
% space between it and a margin note above it, so that the two touch; ours
% isn't raised.
% The optional argument still sets the offset:
% |\begin{marginfigure}[-1.2ex]| is |memoir|'s; on the slides it's ignored.
% As with the notes, when the margin is full, \textsf{marginfix} moves the
% float on to the next page.
%
% \subsection{Tools for code examples}
%
% We also provide two commands for working with code examples: |lstexample| and 
% |runpython|.
%
% \DescribeMacro{\lstexample}
% The |\lstexample| command allows us to typeset the source code of the 
% example.
% For instance, |\lstexample{python}{hello.py}| produces the following:
% \lstexample{python}{hello.py}
%
% We can also pass optional arguments directly to |minted| that is used to 
% typeset the code.
% \begin{example}
% |\lstexample[linenos,highlightlines=4]{python}{hello.py}| produces the 
% following.
% \lstexample[linenos,highlightlines=4]{python}{hello.py}
% \end{example}
%
% \DescribeMacro{\runpython}
% We can also run the example code and include its output using the 
% |\runpython| macro.
% \begin{example}
% For instance, |\runpython[highlightlines=1]{hello.py}| produces the 
% following.
% \runpython[highlightlines=1]{hello.py}
% \end{example}
%
% The optional argument is a key--value list.
% Keys that |\runpython| doesn't know, like |highlightlines| above, are passed 
% on to |minted|.
% The keys it does know control how the program is run.
% \begin{description}
% \item[stdin] One line of input to the program.
%   Repeat the key for several lines: |stdin=4, stdin=10|.
% \item[expect] Wait for this text to appear in the output before typing the 
%   next |stdin| line.
%   Without it, the line is typed after |idle| seconds of silence.
% \item[stdinfile] A file whose contents are used as input.
% \item[args] Command-line arguments to the program, split like a shell 
%   does.
% \item[transcript] Show the run as a terminal session: the command line, 
%   the questions, the typed answers and the output.
%   Shorthand for |tty|, |showcommand| and |chdir|.
% \item[tty] Run the program in a pseudo-terminal, so that it behaves as in a 
%   terminal and the answers we type are echoed.
% \item[echo] Whether the terminal echoes what we type; |echo=false| hides 
%   the answers.
% \item[showcommand] Put the command line first, as |$ python3 hello.py|.
% \item[chdir] Run in the program's own directory, so that the command line 
%   reads |python3 hello.py| rather than |python3 examples/hello.py|.
% \item[commandtext] The text to show as the command line, when it should 
%   differ from the command actually run.
% \item[prompt] The shell prompt in front of the command line, |$ | by 
%   default.
% \item[cwd] The directory to run in.
% \item[shell] Run the argument through the shell, as |\runshell| does; 
%   the argument is then the whole command, so |args| and |interpreter| 
%   don't apply.
% \item[basedir] The directory that file names are relative to, see 
%   |\runbasedir| below.
% \item[interpreter] The interpreter, |python3| by default; it may contain 
%   options, as in |python3 -X utf8|.
% \item[timeout] Seconds until the program is killed, 10 by default.
%   The output so far is kept and a note is added.
% \item[idle] Seconds of silence before the next |stdin| line is typed, 0.5 
%   by default.
% \item[deps] Files that the run depends on, so that a change makes PythonTeX 
%   rerun it.
%   The program itself is always a dependency.
% \item[lang] The language |minted| highlights the output as, |text| by 
%   default.
% \item[fullwidth] The same as the starred form of the command.
% \end{description}
% All file names are relative to the document, or rather to PythonTeX's 
% working directory, also when |cwd| or |chdir| is used; with a base 
% directory, they are relative to that instead.
%
% \begin{example}
% The program |ask.py| asks a question:
% \lstexample{python}{ask.py}
% Then |\runpython[transcript, stdin={4}]{ask.py}| shows what happens in the 
% terminal when we answer 4:
% \runpython[transcript, stdin={4}]{ask.py}
% \end{example}
%
% Without |transcript|, the input goes through a pipe: nothing echoes the 
% answer, and there is no newline after the question.
% \begin{example}
% |\runpython[stdin={4}]{ask.py}| gives
% \runpython[stdin={4}]{ask.py}
% which is what the program prints when reading from a pipe, but not what a 
% student sees in the terminal.
% So use |transcript| for programs that ask questions.
% The pipe is right for programs that read data, like |filter.py|:
% \lstexample{python}{filter.py}
% Here |\runpython[stdin={ett}, stdin={två}]{filter.py}| gives
% \runpython[stdin={ett}, stdin={två}]{filter.py}
% \end{example}
%
% \begin{example}
% Several questions need several lines.
% The program |askmany.py| asks two:
% \lstexample{python}{askmany.py}
% |\runpython[transcript, stdin={Kalle}, stdin={7}]{askmany.py}| gives
% \runpython[transcript, stdin={Kalle}, stdin={7}]{askmany.py}
% If we answer only the first, the program gets end-of-file at the second 
% question, and we see the error just as in the terminal:
% \runpython[transcript, stdin={Kalle}]{askmany.py}
% \end{example}
%
% \begin{example}
% Some programs take arguments instead, like |greet.py|:
% \lstexample{python}{greet.py}
% |\runpython[transcript, args={Kalle}]{greet.py}| gives
% \runpython[transcript, args={Kalle}]{greet.py}
% \end{example}
%
% A program that imports a slow library before it asks its question is a 
% trap: after |idle| seconds of silence we type the answer, the terminal 
% echoes it, and only then does the question appear.
% Use |expect| to wait for the question instead:
% |\runpython[transcript, expect={Antal personer:}, stdin={4}]{slow.py}|.
%
% The values of the keys can't contain a percent sign or a hash, and a 
% backslash in them becomes a control sequence.
% For such input, call the Python function directly, since |\pyc| reads its 
% argument verbatim:
% |\pyc{didactic_shell("python3 p.py", stdin=["50%"], transcript=True)}|.
%
% \DescribeMacro{\runsetup}
% Defaults for all runs in a document can be set with |\runsetup{keys}|, for 
% instance |\runsetup{transcript, timeout=5}|.
%
% \DescribeMacro{\runbasedir}
% A book may include parts that were written to be compiled on their own, 
% each in its own directory, say with 
% |\subimport{../lectures/loops/}{contents.tex}| from the \textsf{import} 
% package.
% The part refers to its programs relative to its own directory, as in 
% |\runpython[transcript, chdir]{examples/loop.py}|.
% With |\runbasedir{../lectures/loops/}| the runs that follow resolve their 
% file names, including |cwd|, |stdinfile| and |deps|, against that 
% directory and run the program there.
% The command is left as it is written, so the command line shown is the 
% same as when the part is compiled on its own.
% A relative base directory is relative to the main document.
%
% With |\runbasedir{auto}|, the base directory follows the \textsf{import} 
% package: each run uses the directory of the file being imported, as 
% |\input| and |\includegraphics| do.
% So one |\runbasedir{auto}| in the preamble of the book is enough.
% The setting is local to the current group, and |\runbasedir{}| turns it 
% off again.
% A single run can also be given the key |basedir|.
% This assumes that PythonTeX runs in the directory of the main document, 
% which is the default: a relative base directory is taken from PythonTeX's 
% working directory, while the paths of \textsf{import} are relative to the 
% document.
% If |\setpythontexworkingdir| moves PythonTeX elsewhere, give the base 
% directory explicitly, relative to that working directory.
%
% The base directory applies to the runs only.
% Code that the document runs in PythonTeX itself, in a |pycode| environment 
% for instance, still runs in PythonTeX's working directory.
% Such code may change directory with |os.chdir|; the \textsf{didactic} 
% functions keep working, since they don't depend on the working directory.
%
% \DescribeMacro{\runshell}
% The |\runshell| macro runs any command through the shell, with the same 
% keys.
% \begin{example}
% \verb+\runshell[transcript]{echo ett två tre | wc -w}+ gives
% \runshell[transcript]{echo ett två tre | wc -w}
% \end{example}
% This is also how we run programs in other languages, for instance
% |\runshell[transcript, cwd=examples, deps={examples/hello.c}]{gcc -o hello hello.c && ./hello}|.
%
% \subsection{Code side by side}
%
% \DescribeMacro{\codebycode}
% Two versions of a program, or of one function in two programs, are best 
% compared side by side: the reader sees at once what differs and what stays 
% the same.
% |\codebycode[opt1]{lang1}{file1}[opt2]{lang2}{file2}| sets two files side 
% by side, with columns as wide as the code needs.
% Each side's options can cut out a part of the file instead:
% |name| a function or class (|name=Person.__eq__| for a method), |lines| a 
% range (|lines=3-10|), |from| and |to| the lines between two texts, 
% |nodocstring| leaves out the docstring, and |label| puts a label above the 
% column, the file name if given without a value.
% Other options go to |minted|.
%
% \begin{example}
% The program |pairgreet1.py| calls |main()| at the end, without the 
% |__name__| guard, and asks for input, so it must not be run while the 
% document is built.
% |\codebycode[name=main,label]{python}{pairgreet1.py}| 
% |[name=main,label]{python}{pairgreet2.py}| reads both programs as text and 
% shows their |main| functions:
% \codebycode[name=main,label]{python}{pairgreet1.py}%
%   [name=main,label]{python}{pairgreet2.py}
% \end{example}
%
% In article mode a pair takes the full width, margin included, and keeps 
% margin notes from being set beside it; on the slides it fills the frame, in 
% a smaller font.
% \DescribeMacro{\codebycodesetup}
% |\codebycodesetup| changes that: |fullwidth=false| keeps pairs within the 
% text width, |slides={fontsize=\footnotesize}| and |notes={...}| set the 
% |minted| options in each mode, |gap| the space between the columns, and 
% |labels| labels every column with its file name.
% On the slides, |slidewidth=frame| lets a pair reach into the frame's side 
% margins, up to half the gap from the edge of the slide, which gives it 
% about a fifth more width with |beamer|'s default margins; |slidewidth=text|, 
% the default, keeps it within the text.
% It only reaches out when the pair is as wide as the frame's text, not in a 
% column or a |minipage|; in a block it reaches beyond the block's 
% background.
% Themes with a sidebar keep the sidebar in the left margin, so don't use it 
% with those.
% A side-by-side contrast in the notes is often wider than the text, so the 
% full width is the default.
% The pairs work in |beamer| frames with or without |fragile|.
% A pair is a |tabular|, so it can't break across pages: cut long code down
% with |name|, |lines| or |from| and |to|.
%
% The columns share the width in proportion to their longest lines, but
% whether the code fits depends on the font.
% A line wider than its column runs into the next one, so \textsf{didactic}
% warns about it in the log, with how much too wide it is, the input line 
% and the page near which it is; on the slides, once for each overlay.
% For |didactic_pair| in a |pycode| environment, the line is where the code 
% was when PythonTeX last ran it, which lags after edits above it.
% Then use a smaller font, shorter lines, or |breaklines| for that side,
% which wraps the long lines.
% On the slides, |beamer|'s |shrink| doesn't help much: |shrink=10| makes the
% frame only 11~percent wider.
% The labels are set in the size that |slides| or |notes| gives the code, 
% so in the notes, where |notes| is empty by default, the labels keep the 
% text's size even if the document's |\setminted| makes the code smaller.
%
% \textsf{didactic} writes each side of a pair to a file
% |didactic_pair_|\meta{hash}|.txt|, and the output of each run to
% |didactic_output_|\meta{hash}|.txt|, in PythonTeX's working directory, the
% document's directory by default.
% These are build output, like the |.aux| file: PythonTeX deletes them when 
% it reruns the code that wrote them, and they belong in |.gitignore|.
%
% A use worth knowing from try-first teaching: let the students try 
% something first, show the second version, and then repeat the first one 
% beside it, so that what changed is seen at a glance.
%
% \DescribeMacro{\runbyrun}
% |\runbyrun[opt1]{file1}[opt2]{file2}| does the same with two runs, in two 
% equal columns; each side takes the keys of |\runpython|, and |shell| makes 
% a side a command run through the shell, as with |\runshell|.
%
% \begin{example}
% Both sides take the same keys as |\runpython|, which contrasts two runs of 
% one program with different input:
% |\runbyrun[transcript, stdin={4}]{ask.py}|%
% |[transcript, stdin={10}]{ask.py}| gives
% \runbyrun[transcript, stdin={4}]{ask.py}[transcript, stdin={10}]{ask.py}
% \end{example}
%
% The runs are done in the order of the document, the left side first, so a 
% run sees what the runs before it did, such as a file they wrote.
%
% \DescribeEnv{didacticpair}
% For several runs on a side, write the pair yourself with the 
% |didacticpair| environment, which the commands above use too.
% |\begin{didacticpair}[runs]{2}| starts a pair of two equal columns, and 
% |&| goes to the next column.
% A column can hold several |\runpython| or |\runshell|; they are done 
% from left to right, and from top to bottom within a column.
% |\didacticpairlabel| sets a label above a column, in a first row ended by 
% |\\|.
% The option |runs| keeps the document's |minted| settings for the output, 
% as |\runbyrun| does; without it, the columns are code, in the size of 
% |\codebycodesetup|.
% The option |line| gives the input line for the warning about code wider 
% than its column; a pair written in the document doesn't need it.
% The argument can also be another number of columns, written out as 
% digits, or a column specification for |tabular|.
%
% \begin{example}
% Writing to a file with |"w"| replaces what it held, and |"a"| appends to 
% it.
% The program |pairwrite.py| writes its second argument to a file, opened 
% with its first argument as the mode, and |pairread.py| prints the file.
% \input{pairruns.tex}
% That pair is
% \inputminted{latex}{pairruns.tex}
% The left column is done first, and the columns share the file, so each 
% column starts by writing the file anew.
% On the slides the output keeps the document's size, which is often too 
% wide for two columns: then set a smaller size with |\setminted| in the 
% frame.
% \end{example}
%
% Which tool when: |\codebycode| for two pieces of code from files, 
% |\runbyrun| for two runs, the |didacticpair| environment for pairs by 
% hand, such as several runs on a side, |didactic_pair| in PythonTeX for 
% code that you have as text or want to prepare yourself (more than two 
% columns too), and |\textbytext| for prose side by side.
% To get a function out of a program, |didactic_extract| reads the program as 
% text and never runs it, which is safe for any program; |didactic_import| 
% and |didactic_source| import the program, which runs it, and are safe only 
% for programs that call their |main| under the |__name__| guard and print 
% nothing at the top level.
%
% Those commands are implemented using a set of Python functions that can be 
% accessed through PythonTeX.
% See \cref{PythonTeXdidactic} for details.
%
% \subsection{Listings across pages}\label{KeepListingsUsage}
%
% In the notes, we keep listings whole where we can.
% That is a practice, not a finding: no study read shows that a listing across
% pages is harder to read (\cref{app:pb-listings}).
% That a function cut in two, or two lines of a program at the foot of a page and
% the rest on the next, are worth avoiding is this package's own view.
% So outside |beamer|, \textsf{didactic} decides where listings may break.
% This holds for every listing set by \textsf{fancyvrb}, which is every
% listing of |minted|: |\lstexample|, |\inputminted|, the |minted|
% environment, the output of |\runpython| and |\runshell|, and the code
% chunks of a literate program woven for |minted|.
% It doesn't hold for chunks that \textsf{noweb} sets itself, without |minted|:
% |keeplines| and |keepends| don't reach those, and an 8-line chunk breaks as it does
% without this package, with one line of code at the foot of a page if that is what
% fits, and no warning.
% Only |keepleadin| and |keepmaxgap| hold for them.
% \DescribeOpt{keeplines}
% \DescribeOpt{keepends}
% \DescribeOpt{keepleadin}
% \begin{description}
% \item[keeplines] A listing of at most this many lines doesn't break; it
%   moves to the next page as a whole, within the limit of |keepmaxgap|: if
%   moving it with what leads into it would leave more empty lines than that,
%   it breaks after all, by the rule of |keepends|, or starts the next page by
%   itself.
%   Unless it wouldn't fit on a page, with a few lines to spare for what is
%   kept with it: lines can wrap (|breaklines|), so that is a matter of its
%   height.
%   Such a listing breaks by the rule of |keepends|, with a warning.
%   The default is 10.
% \item[keepends] A longer listing may break, but leaves at least this many
%   lines on each side of the break, not counting empty lines.
%   The default is 2.
%   If those lines don't fit on a page, for they wrap, they break too, with
%   a warning.
%   Nor does a page start with an empty line of a longer listing: it breaks
%   after the empty line instead, between two parts of the program.
% \item[keepleadin] No page break comes between a listing, or code side by
%   side, and the paragraph
%   before it, which usually leads into it (``It prints:''), just as
%   \TeX\ doesn't break a page before a displayed formula.
%   A colour set for the listing doesn't change that.
%   A margin note or a float on its own between them, after a blank line,
%   does, and so does space that you add with |\vspace|: \LaTeX\ gives a
%   place to break there, whatever comes next.
%   If the two land on different pages that way, \textsf{didactic} warns, with
%   the page and the input line.
%   To keep them together, write a note in the paragraph that leads into the
%   listing, without a blank line, and a float before that paragraph.
%   A break that you ask for doesn't warn, with |\pagebreak| or |\goodbreak|
%   say, and nor does one after a list, a listing or code side by side, which end
%   in a place to break.
%   Ask with those, not with a bare |\penalty|: we don't see that one asked.
%   Before a listing on its own it says nothing, as the page ended at a negative
%   penalty, but before a list around the listing, such as |center|, \TeX\ often ends
%   the page at the list's own penalty instead, the last of breaks that cost the
%   same, and then it warns, and blames a note, a float or space.
%   The start of a list around the listing, such as |center|, gives a place to break
%   too, and that warns, but not right after the end of another list, where nothing
%   leads into it.
%   The header line of a code chunk of \textsf{noweb} stays with its code
%   too.
%   What moves with the listing is at least the last line of that paragraph,
%   which can then stand alone above the listing, since the club and widow
%   penalties stay as the class has them.
%   All this within |keepmaxgap|: if moving them would leave more empty lines
%   than that, the listing can start the next page by itself, as without this
%   option, and if it does a warning says so.
%   The same goes for a list (|itemize|, |enumerate|, |description|, with
%   \textsf{enumitem} too): it doesn't start the next page with what leads into it
%   at the foot of this one, though it may break between its items as ever.
%   Past the limit it starts the next page only if its first item doesn't fit there,
%   and a warning says so.
%   If a page breaks above a lead-in that was tied past the limit, a warning says that
%   as well.
%   The default is |true|.
% \item[keepmaxgap] What moves to the next page to stay together may leave at
%   most this many empty lines at the foot of the page.
%   The lines are lines of the text, and what moves along counts too: the
%   lines of the lead-in, and a heading right before them.
%   If it would leave more, a listing of at most |keeplines| lines breaks
%   there, still with |keepends| lines on each side, and the tie to what leads
%   into it is released.
%   A listing that can't break, since |keepends| asks for more lines than it
%   has or than fit, starts the next page without its lead-in, with a warning.
%   With a limit, the place to break that \textsf{noweb} offers after every
%   code chunk, which also leaves empty space, is dropped.
%   The limit bounds the moves of \textsf{didactic}, not the breaks that \TeX\
%   finds best by itself, which can leave as much with or without this package.
%   Nor does it bound what \textsf{noweb} does without its option |breakcode|:
%   a |\filbreak| after each chunk, which can leave most of a page empty, and
%   there is a warning.
%   Use |\noweboptions{breakcode}|; |\usepackage[breakcode]{noweb}| doesn't set it.
%   The default is 5; 0 turns the limit off.
% \item[keepafterheading] At least this many lines follow a heading on its 
%   page: of text, of a listing, or of a table.
%   If they don't fit, the heading starts the next page.
%   This holds for |\section| and the levels below it, |\paragraph|
%   included, so also for the frame titles in the notes, and for the
%   headings of the blocks (|example|, |exercise|, |solution| and so on),
%   but not for |\chapter|.
%   That usually starts a page.
%   Under the option |article| of \textsf{memoir} it doesn't, and then the limit of
%   |keepmaxgap| holds for it as for any heading, what is tied to it breaks there or
%   warns; it asks no lines of its own, and the first two lines of a paragraph stay
%   with it because \LaTeX\ keeps them.
%   So a chapter followed by a paragraph of one line, whose next paragraph goes to the
%   next page, has one line under it, and that doesn't warn; the tests leave chapters
%   out of the rule that fewer lines than this option asks warn.
%   A table of \textsf{longtable} that starts on the next page, leaving the 
%   heading behind, takes the heading along in the next run.
%   When the page no longer needs that, or with this option off, the heading 
%   stays in the run after, whatever the old |.aux| file says.
%   Moving a heading must also stay within |keepmaxgap|, which wins: then the
%   heading has what \LaTeX\ would give it, the lines that fit if it has more
%   room than this option asks, or the table starts on the next page.
%   A heading is never parted from what follows it, also not a run-in heading from
%   a list or a block, and if the first two lines of that don't fit, or what comes
%   first can't break, it moves whatever the limit, as \LaTeX\ moves it.
%   A listing right under a heading breaks past the limit, by the rule of
%   |keepends|, if it has the lines for that.
%   A list right under a heading has what \LaTeX\ gives it: its first item, also
%   of one line.
%   At |keepafterheading=3|, not the default, a one-line paragraph and a list after it
%   can end up with one line less than \LaTeX\ keeps, since the first lines of the first
%   item are tied; a warning says so.
%   Whenever a heading has too few lines, or moves past the limit, a warning gives
%   the file and the line of the heading and says what the page did.
%   That comes from the pages, so it takes the |.aux| file: with |\nofiles| a warning
%   says that nothing can be told, and in columns one says that a move to the next
%   column can't be.
%   Nothing else keeps a heading from these lines, except a page you end
%   yourself, or a place to break that \LaTeX\ offers between short paragraphs
%   and a list.
%   The default is 2; 0 turns it off.
% \end{description}
% A line counts as empty when it holds nothing but spaces and tabs.
% One that holds only something else that prints nothing, like a |\label| by
% |escapeinside|, counts as a line.
%
% A listing kept whole can still fail to fit on a page, with what is kept
% with it: a tall box in the paragraph before it, or a float at the top of
% the next page, which is placed before \TeX\ gets to the listing.
% Nor can the first |keepends| lines of a longer listing.
% Then that page is overfull, and \textsf{didactic} warns, as it does whenever
% it can't do what these options ask.
% With |keepmaxgap=0|, if what can't part is the whole of the page, \LaTeX\ first ends
% the page before it and tries on an empty one, so that page stays nearly empty as well.
% Give the listing room with a place to break before it, such as |\goodbreak|.
% A line taller than a page can't be kept from running off it, since a page
% can't break within a line: what doesn't fit is lost, and \textsf{didactic} says
% so.
%
% The page that a listing moves away from comes up short, and what happens
% with that space depends on the document.
% With |\raggedbottom| the space stays empty at the foot of the page.
% That is the default of the one-sided documents of the standard classes
% and |memoir|.
% With |\flushbottom|, the default of their two-sided documents, and so of
% |memoir| without options, \TeX\ stretches the page to its full height: the
% space goes to the gaps around headings and between paragraphs, and the log
% has an |Underfull \vbox|.
% If you want the empty foot, put |\raggedbottom| in the preamble of the
% notes; \textsf{didactic} doesn't change it for you.
% When a heading moves, the space stays at the foot whatever you choose, so
% \textsf{memoir}'s |\raggedbottomsection| changes nothing within |keepmaxgap|; it
% matters only for a heading that \LaTeX\ moves past that.
% \textsf{memoir}'s |\sloppybottom| lets a page hold more by shrinking its
% |\topskip|; \textsf{didactic} counts that as room, as \TeX\ does.
% Either way |keeplines| is a trade-off between a listing cut in two and
% empty space, and |keepmaxgap| bounds the empty space: a listing of 10
% lines moves whole only if at most 5 lines of the page are left empty,
% and otherwise breaks as a longer one does.
% The default keeps a short function whole and lets a whole program break.
% Since a longer listing keeps |keepends| lines at each end, one of fewer
% than $2\times3$ lines that aren't empty can't break anyway.
% With a larger font for the code, choose fewer lines.
%
% |keeplines=0, keepends=1, keepleadin=false| comes closest to the breaks of
% \textsf{fancyvrb} itself, which it can't turn into quite the same.
% A page still doesn't start with an empty line of a listing; no option
% changes that.
% And \textsf{fvextra}, which is loaded by |minted|, lets a listing break
% after any line, whatever the penalties: that no longer happens.
% Listings inside a box, such as the code side by side above, can't break
% anyway, and on the slides nothing changes.
% In |multicols| they stay as \textsf{fancyvrb} has them, each with a warning: the
% columns are made later, so we can't know the height a listing should fit.
%
% What warns looks at the page when \TeX\ has decided, for all listings, for code
% side by side and for the chunks of \textsf{noweb}, whether woven for |minted| or
% set by \textsf{noweb} itself, with or without |breakcode|.
% It can't see everything:
% \begin{itemize}
% \item The environment |webcode| of \textsf{noweb} isn't tied to anything and isn't
%   looked at.
% \item Nor is a chunk of \textsf{noweb} with no lines of code.
% \item A listing in a box, such as a |minipage|, is on the page with the box, and the
%   box goes wherever it fits: a blank line before the box parts it from the lead-in
%   with no warning.
% \item A break with no penalty, |\pagebreak[0]| or |\allowbreak|, looks like the break
%   after a note, and warns.
%   Inside a list around the listing, such as |center|, it says nothing if \TeX\ ends
%   the page at the list's own penalty before it instead.
% \item Under |\flushbottom|, as in a document with two sides, code side by side at
%   full width in a |center| can warn after a |\goodbreak|: there a place to break
%   after the start of the pair, where its margin is blocked, makes a page that
%   stretches better than yours.
% \item In two columns, no page is shipped out between them.
%   So when a column ended before a listing even started, the break it looks at is
%   the end of the last page, and a break you asked for there can warn, or one you
%   didn't can stay silent.
%   A listing that makes the column end itself is looked at correctly.
% \item It tells a listing that starts the page from one with something above it by
%   the height of the page, allowing half a line.
%   After a frame of \textsf{fancyvrb} or the header of a chunk, it allows more,
%   since it can't tell what space above them \TeX\ dropped from the top of the page;
%   half the height of a chunk's header and the space above it.
%   Something above the listing lower than that would pass unseen, and so would a
%   header more than twice as tall as |\topskip| be taken for something above it.
% \item If the lead-in ends in space of its own, such as |\addvspace|, right before the
%   listing or the list, and the tie is released, we can't write down the page of its
%   last line without hiding that space from what comes next.
%   So we take the page at the time of the tie, and if \TeX\ then takes that line along
%   to the next page, the warning says that what starts there is parted when it isn't.
% \item Pages are all it looks at for a list, so a list that moves to the next column
%   of |twocolumn|, away from its lead-in, says nothing.
% \item Glue of the primitive |\vskip|, of a fixed size, between the lead-in and a rule
%   before a list: the rule hides it, and a list parted there says nothing.
%   With |\vspace| it warns.
% \item A |\pagebreak| at the end of the lead-in is a break you asked for only if
%   nothing but space follows it in the paragraph; after it, a footnote or a |\label|
%   makes it a break within the lead-in, and what is then parted from it warns.
% \item A place to break after the end of another list doesn't warn, for a list or a
%   listing, wrapped in a |center| or not, or code side by side, as it is one of the
%   places where \LaTeX\ means a page to break.
%   So a display between a lead-in and what follows warns, but a |center| doesn't, as
%   that ends in a list.
% \item That the first line of a list fits is reckoned by a line of the text.
%   If it is taller, \TeX\ may take the lead-in along past the limit, and the warning
%   says that this \emph{can} leave more than |keepmaxgap| lines empty, as it can't
%   tell how many lines went.
% \end{itemize}
%
