%
% \paragraph{Two lines after a heading (\texttt{keepafterheading}).}
% The University of Chicago Press's sample house style, in the 16th edition of
% its \emph{Manual}, says: \enquote{The minimum number of lines of text below a
% subhead at the bottom of a page is two.}\cite[fig.~A.3]{chicago2010manual}
% It is a labelled sample of what a publisher tells its typesetters, not a rule of
% the \emph{Manual}, and nothing shows it was applied; it is two editions old, and
% whether it survives in the 17th and 18th wasn't established.
% The running text, in what was read of the 16th to 18th editions, has no number.
% Thesis manuals say two as well; of what was read, only a W3C draft goes higher,
% with \enquote{two or three}, and gives no source for
% it.\footnote{\Cref{app:pb-lines} has who states what.}
% Plain \LaTeX\ reaches two for a paragraph of two lines or more, and
% \textsf{memoir} is the same.
% For two the support is moderate for a craft rule, for three weak.
% What the option decides is narrower than its name: the estimate before a heading asks
% for one line at either setting, since it can't know what follows, \LaTeX\ keeps two
% lines of a longer paragraph anyway, and a list under a heading has what \LaTeX\ gives
% it.
% So it decides what the chain holds after a first paragraph of one line.
% There, in generated text, at a room of 4 to 4.5 lines 1 stays with that line and 2
% moves, and at 5 lines 2 stays with two lines.
% With |keepafterheading=1| every one of eight real documents has the same words on each
% page, since none has such a heading at the foot of a page.\footnote{\Cref{app:pb-measure}.}
% Those are real documents and not a controlled sample, and eight is few.
% They were built from a snapshot, with their last woven source, some of them older
% than their \textsf{noweb}, and with the output of PythonTeX from before.
% What this option adds to \LaTeX\ at two is the second line under a one-line
% paragraph, as far as a chain of penalties can hold it.
%
% \paragraph{A heading stays with its first paragraph.}
% This has broad support, as practice and not as a measured effect, and \LaTeX\
% does it: its |\@afterheading| forbids the break after the first line.
% We don't break that for anything, not for the limit on empty space either.
%
% \paragraph{At most five empty lines (\texttt{keepmaxgap}).}
% The same sample of the Press is the only source read with a limit for this very
% case, and one handbook was read:
% \enquote{The text page may be up to four lines short if there is no room for a
% subhead at the bottom of a page.}\cite[fig.~A.3]{chicago2010manual}
% So our five is a line more than the only number there is, and that is a choice,
% with a reason that holds for the fonts of the notes, \textsf{memoir} at 10\,pt: a
% |\section| with its two lines fits from a room of 5.27 lines there, a |\subsection|
% from 5.0, and with \textsf{memoir} at 11\,pt from 5.5, with |article| at 10\,pt from
% 5.84.
% Below that a single heading moves in plain \LaTeX\ too, so between four and five
% our limit changes a warning, not the page.
% It changes the page only for a heading over a one-line paragraph or a list, which
% we move, within the limit, and \LaTeX\ doesn't.
% For other adjustments the sample allows one line, on both facing pages, and books
% with facing pages may well want less than we do; thesis manuals set no limit.
% The support is moderate that the heading moves, and limited for the
% amount.\footnote{\Cref{app:pb-gap} has the numbers, which measure different
%   things.}
%
% \paragraph{No fewer lines than \LaTeX\ keeps, but for two things.}
% Staying within the limit could mean keeping one line under a heading where
% \LaTeX\ keeps two or moves it.
% No source read argues, as a principle, for fewer lines to avoid white
% space.\footnote{See \cref{app:pb-gap}.}
% So past the limit we follow \LaTeX, and warn.
% Where \LaTeX\ has the header of a chunk of \textsf{noweb} alone at the foot, as
% a line under a heading, we move the header to its code.
% A listing kept whole has fewer lines than \LaTeX\ gives it, too, which is what
% |keeplines| is for.
%
% \paragraph{Club and widow penalties as the class has them.}
% We set neither: 1000 and 1000 under \textsf{memoir}, 150 and 150 under |article|.
% The rule is the maintainer's: fewer pages, then fewer changes from the defaults.
% On those eight real documents, 813 pages, with the same limits, 1000 and 1000 gave the
% same pages as 1000 and 10000, document by document, and 10000 and 10000 added
% one;\footnote{\Cref{app:pb-measure}.} in generated documents of about 1630 pages 1000
% and 1000 was 4 pages shorter than 1000 and 10000, 1627 against 1631.
% Three of those are in plain \textsf{memoir} without this package, and one is a
% document woven for |minted| whose last page holds about a line.
% Under |article|, lifting 150 to 1000 costs pages in generated text, 607 against
% 610 for six documents of each kind, so we don't do that either.
% The price is stated too: on those real documents 8 last lines alone at the top of a
% page, against none at a widow penalty of 10000, and 3 first lines alone at the foot,
% as at 10000.
% \emph{The Chicago Manual of Style} says more: in three editions, a page should not
% \enquote{begin with the last line of a paragraph unless it is full measure}, while
% \enquote{A page can, however, end with the first line of a new
% paragraph}.\cite[§2.121]{chicago2024manual}
% That stronger rule on last lines we don't follow.
% Many of those read treat the two the same way, as craft.\footnote{%
%   \Cref{app:pb-penalties} has them and the \TeX\ behind the numbers.}
% The sources speak of the outcome and not of penalties, so they say nothing of
% the number.
% The remedy for a lone line that bothers you is the one that
% \textcite[p.~250]{mittelbach2018forlorn} gives: \enquote{Thus you are better off
% allowing widows and orphans (by changing the parameter values) and manually
% correcting them in one way or another}.
% The value of 1000 is \textsf{memoir}'s, whose only stated reason is
% \enquote{we want stronger discouragement}.\cite{wilson2025memoirsrc}
% A penalty of 10000 would remove them, at the price of \enquote{partly empty pages},
% and the manual of \textsf{memoir} says that it \enquote{may well result in some odd
% looking pages, especially if \cs{raggedbottom} is not
% used}.\cite[§3.5]{wilson2025memoir}
%
% \paragraph{The lead-in stays with its listing (\texttt{keepleadin}), within the limit.}
% The manual of \textsf{memoir} says, of lists, that \enquote{it can be annoying
% if there is a page break between the introductory words and the first item}, and
% offers |\noprelistbreak|, per list, off by default.\cite[§18.7]{wilson2025memoir}
% The Press's sample asks for two text lines above a list and doesn't name the
% lead-in; that those would normally include it is the review's
% reading.\cite[fig.~A.3]{chicago2010manual}
% The other support is by analogy, and the defaults of \LaTeX\ and \textsf{memoir}
% go the other way: they give a bonus for a break before a list.
% No source read speaks of a listing's lead-in.\footnote{%
%   \Cref{app:pb-lists} has these, and says that the evidence doesn't settle it; see
%   also \cref{app:pb-listings}.}
% Weak support can't outweigh a page with a hole in it, so the tie holds only within
% |keepmaxgap|, and past it the listing starts the next page as it would in plain
% \LaTeX, with a warning.
%
% \paragraph{Short listings whole, ten lines (\texttt{keeplines}).}
% No source read gives a length below which a listing stays whole, and 10 is ours.
% The nearest figure is \textsf{CWEB}'s, whose code should be \enquote{about a dozen
% lines long, so that it is easily comprehensible as a unit}, which is about the unit
% and not about pages.\cite{knuth2025cweb}
% \textsf{noweb} keeps every chunk on a page by default, so our limit is narrower than
% its default; |breakcode| gives that up.\cite{ramsey2026noweb}
% \textsf{fancyvrb} and |minted| leave |samepage| off, \textsf{listings} breaks, and
% \textsf{tcolorbox} doesn't break unless asked.\footnote{\Cref{app:pb-listings} has
%   these and what they say.}
%
% \paragraph{Two lines at each end (\texttt{keepends}).}
% Two counts were read and both are two: the same sample of the Press, two lines of
% \enquote{a separate text element}, which it doesn't say of code, and
% \textsf{tcolorbox}'s |lines before break|, for a box and not for
% code.\cite[fig.~A.3]{chicago2010manual}\cite{sturm2025tcolorbox}
% So we say two, by analogy, as the maintainer's choice: neither is about code.
%
% \paragraph{A listing right under a heading, past the limit.}
% A heading stays with what follows it, and the limit holds too, so the heading stays
% and the listing breaks, with a warning, by the same rule of two lines at each end.
% Below that all moves, as \LaTeX\ moves it when nothing can part from the heading.
% So a listing of three lines moves, and leaves up to about six lines empty, with a
% warning, where plain \LaTeX\ keeps two of its lines and leaves one alone at the top
% of the next page: a lone line of code is what the rule is there to avoid.
%
% \paragraph{A list stays with what leads into it, against \LaTeX.}
% We forbid the break before the first item, under |keepleadin| and within
% |keepmaxgap|; \cs{@beginparpenalty} is $-51$ in the standard classes and
% \textsf{memoir}, a bonus for that very break.\footnote{\Cref{app:pb-lists}.}
% The manual of \textsf{memoir} calls the break annoying while its own penalty
% encourages it, and offers |\noprelistbreak| for one list at a time; the evidence
% is thin, and the choice is the maintainer's: rather have the first item or two on
% this page and break in the middle.
% So only that break changes, and between the items a page breaks as ever.
% Past the limit it can start the next page, with a warning, but only if its first
% item doesn't fit: where it does, $-51$ would part them for no reason, on a page
% that stretches.
% On those eight real documents it costs no page, 813 either way, and the words on each
% page are those of that penalty alone at 10000.\footnote{\Cref{app:pb-measure}.}
% Without it, at $-51$, 14 of the 218 lists that could be parted from what leads into
% them are, 13 from a real lead-in, 8 of those in one document.
% With it 7 lead-ins move, and the page they leave has up to 3.9 lines empty; pages with
% three lines or more empty go from 113 to 114 of the 675 text pages logged, and those
% with more than five from 65 to 64.
%
% \paragraph{A list right under a heading follows \LaTeX.}
% Its first item stays under the heading even when one line fits.
% That is fewer than the sample's two lines, and goes against the two-line rule, which
% stays for a heading over a paragraph.
% It is the maintainer's choice, for leaving a default where it is; it wasn't measured
% to help: at commit 9083677, letting that item stay with one line changed no word on
% any page of those eight documents.
%
% \paragraph{Reckoning as \TeX\ does, and warnings.}
% These are ours, not from the sources.
% Layout is correct by construction, and what can't be guaranteed warns, and says
% what the page did.
% So room counts what the page can shrink, since \TeX\ does, and whatever depends on
% how the page came out is told from the pages themselves, at the end of the run.
%
% \paragraph{\textsf{noweb}.}
% Under |breakcode| we drop the break that \textsf{noweb} offers after a chunk with
% |\nwdocspar|.
% We measured that on twelve generated documents, in \textsf{memoir}'s |article|, with
% the mix of chunk lengths of real ones, of about 210 pages.\footnote{%
%   \Cref{app:pb-measure}; \texttt{measure-flow} in the sources has the generator.}
% With the chunks that \textsf{noweb} sets, keeping it leaves at most 7.6 empty lines,
% 33 pages with three or more and 4 with five or more; dropping it leaves 5.6, 20 and 1,
% for 19 more breaks inside chunks; plain \textsf{noweb} with |breakcode| has 6.2, 31
% and 2.
% All of that is this break: those chunks can break anywhere, and the limit changes one
% page's count.
% With the chunks woven for |minted|, which most decks are and which we keep together,
% the limit does the larger part: with it and with this break kept we leave at most 6.2,
% without either 8.9, with this break dropped and no limit 10.5, and with both 4.5, 17
% pages with three or more and a mean of 0.71 a page, where plain has 6.2, 28 and 0.96.
% The same documents without |breakcode| leave up to 41 lines empty, which is why
% we warn about that.
%
% \StopEventually{}
%
% \section{Implementation}
%
% Let's start with the options.
%
% \subsection{Options}
%
% We have a few negative options, that is, if specified we don't want to do 
% them.
% This means that we'll need to use conditionals.
% So each option definition consists first of a |\newif| then setting it to 
% true, and finnally, a |\DeclareOption|.
% The body of the |\DeclareOption| then sets the |\if| to false.
% After all the options are declared we'll use |\ProcessOptions|.
% After that we can have all code actually doing the work, all wrapped in |\if| 
% statements.
%
% \begin{option}{nobeamer}
% \begin{option}{nobeamertheme}
% \begin{option}{noarticle}
% This option is used to disable the |beamer| specific parts of the package.
% Meaning that we don't customize |beamer| even when we find that it is loaded.
% Likewise, |noarticle| leaves the article mode of |beamer|
% (|beamerarticle|) as it is.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newif\if@didactic@beamer
\@ifclassloaded{beamer}{\@didactic@beamertrue}{\@didactic@beamerfalse}
\DeclareOptionX{nobeamer}{\@didactic@beamerfalse}
\newif\if@didactic@article
\@ifclassloaded{beamer}{\@didactic@articlefalse}{\@didactic@articletrue}
\newif\if@didactic@beamertheme
\@didactic@beamerthemetrue
\DeclareOptionX{nobeamertheme}{\@didactic@beamerthemefalse}
\DeclareOptionX{noarticle}{\@didactic@articlefalse}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{nomemoir}
% This option is used to disable the |memoir| specific parts of the package.
% Meaning that we don't customize |memoir| even when we find that it is loaded.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newif\if@didactic@memoir
\@ifclassloaded{memoir}{\@didactic@memoirtrue}{\@didactic@memoirfalse}
\DeclareOptionX{nomemoir}{\@didactic@memoirfalse}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{notheorems}
% This option is used to disable defining the usual environments provided by 
% |amsmath|: definition, theorem, etc.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newif\if@didactic@theorems
\@didactic@theoremstrue
\DeclareOptionX{notheorems}{\@didactic@theoremsfalse}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{inner}
% \begin{option}{outer}
% \begin{option}{top}
% \begin{option}{bottom}
% We also have some key--value options to control the margins.
% The golden ratio is \((1 + \sqrt 5) / 2\), which is approximately 
% 1.61803398875.
% The ratio of the inner and outer margins should be 1.61803398875.
% However, to fit more margin notes, we'll use the golden ratio squared.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newlength\@didactic@margin@inner
\setlength\@didactic@margin@inner{23mm}
\newlength\@didactic@margin@outer
\setlength\@didactic@margin@outer{60.2mm}
\newlength\@didactic@margin@top
\setlength\@didactic@margin@top{30mm}
\newlength\@didactic@margin@bottom
\setlength\@didactic@margin@bottom{48.5mm}
\DeclareOptionX{inner}{\setlength\@didactic@margin@inner{#1}}
\DeclareOptionX{outer}{\setlength\@didactic@margin@outer{#1}}
\DeclareOptionX{top}{\setlength\@didactic@margin@top{#1}}
\DeclareOptionX{bottom}{\setlength\@didactic@margin@bottom{#1}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{marginparmargin}
% We also have an option to set the marginpar margin.
% We simply pass this on to |memoir| and keep a record ourselves too.
% We know that the default in |memoir| is |outer|.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@marginparmargin{outer}
\@ifclassloaded{memoir}
  {\marginparmargin{outer}\sidecapmargin{outer}}
  {\relax}
\DeclareOptionX{marginparmargin}{%
  \renewcommand\@didactic@marginparmargin{#1}
}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{footnotereuse}
% \begin{option}{draft}
% The |footnotereuse| option chooses how citation footnotes are reused, see 
% below.
% Unless it's given, it depends on |draft|: a draft gives every citation its 
% own footnote, to save passes.
% The package options |draft| and |final| win; otherwise we ask the 
% \textsf{ifdraft} package, which looks at the class options.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@fnreusemode{}
\newcommand\@didactic@draft{}
\DeclareOptionX{footnotereuse}{\renewcommand\@didactic@fnreusemode{#1}}
\DeclareOptionX{draft}{\renewcommand\@didactic@draft{draft}}
\DeclareOptionX{final}{\renewcommand\@didactic@draft{final}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{notepriority}
% The |notepriority| option is passed on to \textsf{marginfix}'s 
% |\marginnotepriority| (version 1.5 and later), see below.
% We default to |split|: this package puts footnotes and commentary in the 
% same margin, and a footnote must stay with its mark.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@notepriority{split}
\DeclareOptionX{notepriority}{\renewcommand\@didactic@notepriority{#1}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{notebesidefloats}
% The |notebesidefloats| option is passed on to \textsf{marginfix}'s 
% |\marginnotebesidefloats| (version 1.5 and later), see below.
% We default to |reserve|, \textsf{marginfix}'s own default: with |use|, a 
% float that reaches into the margin in a way \textsf{marginfix} can't see 
% gets notes set over it, without a warning, and documents that worked 
% before mustn't change like that when this package is updated.
% A document whose floats stay in the text block can choose |use|.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@notebesidefloats{reserve}
\DeclareOptionX{notebesidefloats}{%
  \renewcommand\@didactic@notebesidefloats{#1}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{noteoverflow}
% The |noteoverflow| option is passed on to \textsf{marginfix}'s 
% |\marginnoteoverflow| (version 1.6 and later), see below.
% We default to |break|, unlike \textsf{marginfix}: a footnote in the 
% margin must stay with its mark, and when the margin of a page can't hold 
% the notes of its text even after |notepriority| has moved the commentary, 
% the text has to give way.
% That takes several runs, each moving text that the next run may have to move 
% again, so with |draft| the default is |carry|, as with the other 
% options that take runs; see the option |draft|, below, for where that is 
% decided.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@noteoverflow{}
\DeclareOptionX{noteoverflow}{%
  \renewcommand\@didactic@noteoverflow{#1}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{marginfloatorder}
% The |marginfloatorder| option chooses between a margin float beside its text
% (|beside|, the default) and figure numbers in page order (|inorder|), see
% below.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@marginfloatorder{beside}
\DeclareOptionX{marginfloatorder}{%
  \renewcommand\@didactic@marginfloatorder{#1}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{sidecapalign}
% The |sidecapalign| option says where the contents of a float with a side 
% caption go, see below.
% It's |caption| by default: next to the caption.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@sidecapalign{caption}
\DeclareOptionX{sidecapalign}{%
  \renewcommand\@didactic@sidecapalign{#1}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{keeplines}
% \begin{option}{keepends}
% \begin{option}{keepleadin}
% The options |keeplines|, |keepends| and |keepleadin| say how listings
% break across pages, see \cref{KeepListings}.
% A listing of at most |keeplines| lines doesn't break, unless moving it would 
% leave more empty lines than |keepmaxgap|; a longer one, or one that breaks 
% for that reason, leaves at least |keepends| lines on each side of a break, 
% and with |keepleadin| no page break comes between a listing and the text that 
% leads into it.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcount\@didactic@keeplines
\@didactic@keeplines=10
\newcount\@didactic@keepends
\@didactic@keepends=2
\newif\if@didactic@keepleadin
\@didactic@keepleadintrue
\DeclareOptionX{keeplines}{\@didactic@keeplines=#1\relax}
\DeclareOptionX{keepends}{\@didactic@keepends=#1\relax}
\DeclareOptionX{keepleadin}[true]{%
  \IfStrEqCase{#1}{%
    {true}{\@didactic@keepleadintrue}%
    {false}{\@didactic@keepleadinfalse}%
  }[\PackageError{didactic}{Unknown value `#1' for keepleadin}
      {Use true or false.}]}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
%
% \begin{option}{keepafterheading}
% \begin{option}{keepmaxgap}
% |keepafterheading| is the number of lines that follow a heading on its 
% page, and |keepmaxgap| the number of empty lines that keeping a listing 
% together may leave at the foot of a page, see \cref{KeepListings}.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcount\@didactic@keepafterheading
\@didactic@keepafterheading=2
\newcount\@didactic@keepmaxgap
\@didactic@keepmaxgap=5
\DeclareOptionX{keepafterheading}{\@didactic@keepafterheading=#1\relax}
\DeclareOptionX{keepmaxgap}{\@didactic@keepmaxgap=#1\relax}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
% \end{option}
%
% Now that we've declared the options we can process them.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\ProcessOptionsX\relax
%    \end{macrocode}
% Then we can settle the reuse of footnotes: the number of changes of mind 
% before a citation reserves space, or no reuse at all.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\RequirePackage{ifdraft}
\ifdefempty{\@didactic@draft}
  {\ifoptiondraft{\renewcommand\@didactic@draft{draft}}{}}{}
\ifdefempty{\@didactic@fnreusemode}{%
  \ifdefstring{\@didactic@draft}{draft}
    {\renewcommand\@didactic@fnreusemode{off}}
    {\renewcommand\@didactic@fnreusemode{compact}}}{}
\newif\if@didactic@noteoverflowgiven
\ifdefempty{\@didactic@noteoverflow}{%
  \ifdefstring{\@didactic@draft}{draft}
    {\renewcommand\@didactic@noteoverflow{carry}}
    {\renewcommand\@didactic@noteoverflow{break}}%
}{\@didactic@noteoverflowgiventrue}
\newif\if@didactic@fnreuseoff
\newcount\@didactic@fnthreshold
\IfStrEqCase{\@didactic@fnreusemode}{%
  {fast}{\@didactic@fnthreshold\@ne}%
  {compact}{\@didactic@fnthreshold\tw@}%
  {off}{\@didactic@fnreuseofftrue}%
}[\PackageError{didactic}{Unknown value `\@didactic@fnreusemode'
    for footnotereuse}{Use fast, compact or off.}]
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% \subsection{Customizing \texttt{beamer}}
%
% If |beamer| is loaded we want to customize it.
% However, only if the user hasn't disabled this with the |nobeamer| option.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\if@didactic@beamer
%    \end{macrocode}

% If we use |beamer| we want to use the Berlin theme.
% This theme guides the viewer very nicely, how must is left and where we are
% in the presentation.
% (The same is true for the presenter.)
%    \begin{macrocode}
\if@didactic@beamertheme
  \usetheme{Berlin}
%    \end{macrocode}
% However, we want to customize the footline.
% We want to add two lines in the footline.
% We want to add the author (left) and institute (right) to the first line.
% Then we add the title (left) and page number (right) to the bottom line.
%    \begin{macrocode}
  \setbeamertemplate{footline}%{miniframes theme}
  {%
    \begin{beamercolorbox}[colsep=1.5pt]{upper separation line foot}
    \end{beamercolorbox}
    \begin{beamercolorbox}[ht=2.5ex,dp=1.125ex,%
      leftskip=.3cm,rightskip=.3cm plus1fil]{author in head/foot}%
      \leavevmode{\usebeamerfont{author in head/foot}\insertshortauthor}%
      \hfill%
      {\usebeamerfont{institute in head/foot}%
        \usebeamercolor[fg]{institute in head/foot}\insertshortinstitute}%
    \end{beamercolorbox}%
    \begin{beamercolorbox}[ht=2.5ex,dp=1.125ex,%
      leftskip=.3cm,rightskip=.3cm plus1fil]{title in head/foot}%
      {\usebeamerfont{title in head/foot}\insertshorttitle}%
      \hfill%
      \insertframenumber%
    \end{beamercolorbox}%
    \begin{beamercolorbox}[colsep=1.5pt]{lower separation line foot}
    \end{beamercolorbox}
  }
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% We also want to set the transparency of the covered items.
% We also want to set the bibliography item to text, we need this to get proper 
% references (not icons) in the bibliography.
% (We need that for certain bibliography styles.)
%    \begin{macrocode}
  \setbeamercovered{transparent}
  \setbeamertemplate{bibliography item}{\relax}
\fi
