%
% We also want to adapt the citation commands of a few packages to use 
% footnotes.
% For these, we check if the package is loaded, if it is, we do the changes.
% We don't load the packages ourselves, we assume the user has done that.
% This also means that the |didactic| package should be loaded last.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\@ifpackageloaded{biblatex}{%
  \ExecuteBibliographyOptions{%
    autocite=footnote,
    singletitle=false,
    maxbibnames=99,
    isbn=false,doi=false,url=false
  }
  % from https://tex.stackexchange.com/a/374059/17418
  \DeclareCiteCommand{\fullauthorcite}
    {\usebibmacro{prenote}}
    {\usedriver
      {\setcounter{maxnames}{99}% use up to 99 authors
        \DeclareNameAlias{sortname}{default}}
      {\thefield{entrytype}}}
    {\multicitedelim}
    {\usebibmacro{postnote}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% Now we want to reuse footnotes for citations that repeat on the same page: a
% second \cs{footcite} of the same reference on the same page should reuse the
% first footnote's number (a bare \cs{footnotemark}) instead of emitting a new
% footnote, while a citation on a fresh page gets a fresh footnote.
%
% The hard part is knowing \emph{which} page we are on. The page counter
% (\cs{c@page}) is asynchronous --- it is only authoritative inside the output
% routine --- so testing it at the point of the citation misfires near page
% breaks. We instead take the absolute page number from the |.aux| file via
% |zref|'s |abspage| property. That value is written by the previous run, so it
% is one compilation behind and settles on the second pass; but, unlike
% \cs{c@page}, it is reliable.
%
% For every \cs{footcite} we drop a uniquely named |zref| label at the point of
% citation, and every footnote we make gets a label inside its text. The two
% can be on different pages: with footnotes in the margin, \textsf{marginfix}
% moves a note that doesn't fit to the next page, and a mark must not point to
% a note on another page. A citation's \emph{reuse identity} is its entry key
% together with its pre- and postnote, so |\footcite[see][p.~5]{A}| reuses a
% matching earlier one while |\footcite[cf.][p.~7]{A}| gets its own footnote;
% with no notes this reduces to the bare key. For each identity we remember the
% footnote number and the label in the text of the last real footnote, and a
% later citation of that identity reuses the number iff that footnote is
% printed in the same \emph{reuse group} as the citation. It also reuses it
% when the two marks are in the same group, even if the earlier footnote was
% moved on: our own footnote would come after it, so it would be moved at least
% as far, and it would only repeat the earlier one.
%
% The reuse group is normally just the page (|abspage|). Under |twoside|, though,
% the two pages of an open spread --- a verso (even |abspage|) on the left and
% the recto (odd |abspage|) on its right --- are read together, so a footnote on
% the verso may be reused on the recto. We therefore group the pages of a spread,
% \{2,3\}, \{4,5\}, \dots\ (page~1 sits alone), by mapping an odd |abspage| down
% to the even page it faces. The recto/verso parity is simply the parity of
% |abspage| (odd = recto), so we need no |ifoddpage|; we only test \cs{if@twoside}
% to decide whether to group at all. (We deliberately avoid \cs{numexpr} division
% for this: it \emph{rounds}, so 3/2 would give~2 and split the \{2,3\} spread.)
%
% Deciding from the previous pass has a catch: the decision changes the 
% layout.
% A citation that gets its own footnote puts a note in the margin and a 
% different number in the text, which can move the page breaks, which can 
% move the citation onto another page, which reverses the decision.
% Then the passes never settle: the decision flips on every other pass.
% This happens in long documents with many citations, like a book made of 
% several lectures.
% Neither decision is right for such a citation: with its own footnote it 
% lands on the page of the earlier one, so the note is a duplicate; with the 
% mark it lands on another page, so the mark points to a note elsewhere.
%
% So we take the layout out of the decision.
% Each citation that could reuse a footnote writes its decision to the |.aux| 
% file.
% As soon as a citation changes its decision (with |footnotereuse=fast|), 
% or when it changes it back again (with |compact|), it may be flipping, and 
% from then on it \emph{reserves} the space of its own footnote, whatever it 
% decides: when it reuses the earlier footnote, it prints the mark, padded to 
% the width its own mark would have, and an invisible note of exactly the 
% size of its own note.
% Then both decisions give the same layout, and the decision settles on the 
% right one.
% By default we wait to see it flip back: since \textsf{marginfix} records 
% the anchor pages by place in the source, that costs only one pass, and it 
% leaves no blanks that wait for an edit; see the evaluation below.
% (At first we didn't wait, since then waiting cost twice the passes.)
% The invisible note has the same structure as the real one, not just the 
% same size: the same lines, glue and penalties, so that nothing that looks 
% at the list, like \textsf{marginfix} or the page builder, can tell them 
% apart.
% (In our tests one empty box of the same size gave the same result, but it 
% offers no places to break.)
% The one difference left is the footnote counter, which only the own 
% footnote steps; with old-style figures even that changes the height of 
% lines.
% If a citation still flips while reserving space, changing its decision 
% back again in the next pass, we give up and print its own footnote, and a 
% warning says that it repeats one on the same page.
% As soon as its own footnote is the right decision anyway, it goes back to 
% reserving space, which then prints the same.
% Only changes in consecutive passes count: a pass without a change resets 
% the count, so that the changes that editing the text brings about don't add 
% up.
% So in the last pass, a mark always points to a footnote on its page, or 
% to one that the margin moved on from a mark on the same page (where its own 
% footnote would have gone too), and a repeated footnote is never silent.
% We recognise a citation across passes by an MD5 sum of its reuse identity 
% and its number among the citations of that identity, so that an edit only 
% affects the state of later citations of the same source.
% A citation reserves space only while it reuses a footnote: once its own 
% footnote has been the decision for a pass, it goes back to normal, since 
% then both print the same. So the reserved space doesn't grow with edits.
%
% We evaluated this on a course book, as it was on 26 September 2026, made of 
% three lectures with the \textsf{import} package: |memoir| with footnotes in the margin, placed by 
% \textsf{marginfix}, 188 pages in the teacher's edition (with the notes of 
% this package) and 185 in the student's, with 162 citations that could 
% reuse a footnote.
% We compiled from no |.aux| file (clean), and then again after inserting a 
% paragraph with two citations early in the first lecture (edit), counting 
% the runs of |pdflatex| until the |.aux| file no longer changed, including 
% the run that shows it.
% In the last run we counted the marks that point to a footnote printed on 
% another page (\emph{wrong}), the footnotes printed twice on a page 
% (\emph{repeated}), the blanks, and the citations that had given up.
% \begin{center}
% \begin{tabular}{llcccc}
%   \hline
%   & & runs, teacher's/student's & wrong, repeated & blanks & given up \\
%   \hline
%   clean & |fast|    & 7/7   & 0, 0 & 4 & 0 \\
%   clean & |compact| & 14/14 & 0, 0 & 1 & 0 \\
%   clean & |off|     & 3/3   & ---  & 0 & --- \\
%   edit  & |fast|    & 5/5   & 0, 0 & 4 & 0 \\
%   edit  & |compact| & 8/10  & 0, 0 & 2 & 0 \\
%   \hline
% \end{tabular}
% \end{center}
% With |off|, repeated footnotes are expected; that is what the reuse avoids.
% A clean build with |fast| thus needs more runs than \textsf{latexmk} allows 
% by default (5).
%
% We evaluated it again on 8 October 2026, on the student's edition of the 
% same book, which had grown to 16 lectures and 603 pages with 339 
% citations, built with the book's own |make| (so the first run has no 
% bibliography yet).
% Before, \textsf{marginfix} recorded the page of each note's callout under 
% the note's number in the document; a citation that changed between reusing 
% a footnote and its own added or removed a note, which gave every later 
% note its neighbour's page and held it back to the wrong page, which in 
% turn moved notes and changed the minds of other citations.
% With the pages recorded under where the note is called out in the source, 
% and with |fast| no longer reserving space when a citation changes to its 
% own footnote (see below):
% \begin{center}
% \begin{tabular}{lcccc}
%   \hline
%   & runs, clean & runs, edit & wrong, repeated & blanks \\
%   \hline
%   |fast|, before    & 14 & 15 & 0, 0 & 0 \\
%   |compact|, before & 18 & 33 & 0, 0 & 0 \\
%   |fast|            & 6  & 4  & 0, 0 & 0 \\
%   |compact|         & 7  & 4  & 0, 0 & 0 \\
%   \hline
% \end{tabular}
% \end{center}
% No citation gave up, and the two made the same decisions for every 
% citation; |compact|'s extra run only resets its count of changes.
% They differ in the blanks when many pages move at once: with the |.aux| 
% file deleted and the bibliography kept, the first runs see some 20 pages 
% too few, about 40 citations change their minds when the pages come back, 
% and |fast| kept 19 blanks of 15--32\,pt where |compact| kept none; 18 of 
% them were still there after an edit.
% So |compact| is the default: one run more for a margin without blanks 
% that wait for an edit.
%
% The same book shaped the design:
% \begin{itemize}
% \item Deciding from the previous pass alone never settled: the runs 
%   alternated between two states.
% \item Holding a flipping citation at its own footnote settled, but could 
%   print a footnote twice on a page; reserving space is what avoids that.
% \item Deciding by the page of the earlier mark instead of where its 
%   footnote is printed let one mark point to a footnote that 
%   \textsf{marginfix} had moved to the next page.
% \item Letting a citation that reserves space and reuses a footnote step the 
%   footnote counter anyway, to remove the last difference between the two 
%   decisions, changed none of the results, so we don't skip numbers.
% \item Counting changes across edits instead of in consecutive passes made 
%   one edit leave 14 (|fast|) and 43 (|compact|) citations given up, where 
%   none are needed.
% \item Keeping citations reserving space after their own footnote was the 
%   decision didn't change the blanks over four edits in a row, but left 41 
%   citations reserving space instead of 4, each a blank waiting for an edit 
%   that makes it reuse a footnote.
% \end{itemize}
%    \begin{macrocode}
\RequirePackage{zref-abspage,zref-user}
\RequirePackage{pdftexcmds}
\newcount\@didactic@fnpa     % abspage of the previous footnote
\newcount\@didactic@fnpm     % abspage of the mark of the previous footnote
\newcount\@didactic@fnpb     % abspage of the current citation
\newcount\@didactic@fnga     % reuse group of the previous footnote
\newcount\@didactic@fngb     % reuse group of the current citation
\newif\if@didactic@fnreuse
% \@didactic@fnabspage{<label>}: the abspage of a zref label, 0 if unresolved
\newcommand\@didactic@fnabspage[1]{\zref@extractdefault{#1}{abspage}{0}}
% \@didactic@fntogroup<count>: collapse the page in <count> to its reuse group ---
% the page itself, or, under twoside, the verso page of its open spread (an odd
% abspage mapped down to the even page it faces).
\def\@didactic@fntogroup#1{\if@twoside\ifodd#1 \advance#1\m@ne \fi\fi}
\newdimen\@didactic@fnmarkwd
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% Each citation that could reuse a footnote records its state in the |.aux| 
% file, as
% |\@didactic@footcitestate{<serial>}{<decision>}{<mode>}{<changes>}|.
% The serial is the MD5 sum of the reuse identity and the number of the 
% citation among those of the same identity, so that editing the text only 
% affects later citations of the same source.
% The decision is |r| (reuse), |n| (new footnote) or |u| (new footnote, since 
% a page was not yet resolved, which doesn't count as a decision).
% The mode is |0| (normal), |1| (reserving space) or |2| (given up), and the 
% changes are those of the decision in the mode.
% The line only changes when the decision or the mode does, so it costs no 
% extra pass once the decisions have settled.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\def\@didactic@footcitestate#1#2#3#4{\csgdef{@didactic@fnst@#1}{{#2}{#3}{#4}}}
\AtBeginDocument{%
  \if@filesw
    \immediate\write\@auxout{%
      \string\providecommand\string\@didactic@footcitestate[4]{}}%
  \fi}
\def\@didactic@fnzero{0}
\def\@didactic@fnone{1}
\def\@didactic@fntwo{2}
\def\@didactic@fnu{u}
\def\@didactic@fnr{r}
\def\@didactic@fnn{n}
% \@didactic@fnread{<decision>}{<mode>}{<changes>}
\def\@didactic@fnread#1#2#3{%
  \def\@didactic@fnlast{#1}%
  \def\@didactic@fnmode{#2}%
  \@didactic@fnchanges=#3\relax}
\newcount\@didactic@fnchanges
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% The macro |\@didactic@fnhold| counts the changes of decision in 
% consecutive passes and switches to reserving space after 
% |\@didactic@fnthreshold| of them (one for |fast|, two for |compact|), gives 
% up after two more, and records the state in the |.aux| file.
% A pass without a change resets the count, so only flipping counts, not 
% changes spread over edits.
% We record the decision as it comes out of the pages; when we have given up, 
% we print our own footnote instead.
% When that decision is our own footnote anyway, giving up and reserving space 
% print the same, so we go back to reserving space.
% And when it has been our own footnote for a pass, reserving space and the 
% normal mode print the same, so we go back to normal.
% That way only citations that reuse a footnote reserve space, and reserving 
% doesn't pile up over edits.
%
% With |fast|, a change to our own footnote neither counts nor makes us 
% reserve space.
% Our own footnote prints the same either way, and if the citation changes 
% back to reusing the footnote in the next pass, that change makes it reserve 
% space, before it prints anything different.
% Counting it would only rewrite the state in the pass after, to go back to 
% normal, and a pass that changes nothing but that state costs another pass 
% to see that nothing changes.
% (|compact| has to count it, since it waits for the second change.)
%    \begin{macrocode}
\def\@didactic@fnhold{%
  \edef\@didactic@fnnow{%
    \ifnum\@didactic@fnpa<\@ne u\else\ifnum\@didactic@fnpb<\@ne u\else
      \if@didactic@fnreuse r\else n\fi\fi\fi}%
  \let\@didactic@fnlast\@empty
  \def\@didactic@fnmode{0}%
  \@didactic@fnchanges\z@
  \ifcsdef{@didactic@fnst@\@didactic@fnserial}{%
    \expandafter\expandafter\expandafter\@didactic@fnread
      \csname @didactic@fnst@\@didactic@fnserial\endcsname}{}%
  \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnnow}{\@didactic@fnu}{%
    % no decision this pass: keep the last one
    \ifdefempty{\@didactic@fnlast}{}{\let\@didactic@fnnow\@didactic@fnlast}}{%
    \ifdefempty{\@didactic@fnlast}{}{%
      \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnlast}{\@didactic@fnu}{}{%
        \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnnow}{\@didactic@fnlast}
          {\@didactic@fnchanges\z@% held for a pass
           \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnnow}{\@didactic@fnn}
             {\ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnmode}{\@didactic@fnone}
                {\def\@didactic@fnmode{0}}{}}{}}%
          {\advance\@didactic@fnchanges\@ne
           \ifcase\@didactic@fnmode\relax
             \ifnum\@didactic@fnthreshold=\@ne
               \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnnow}{\@didactic@fnn}
                 {\@didactic@fnchanges\z@}{}%
             \fi
             \ifnum\@didactic@fnchanges<\@didactic@fnthreshold\else
               \def\@didactic@fnmode{1}\@didactic@fnchanges\z@\fi
           \or
             \ifnum\@didactic@fnchanges>\@ne
               \def\@didactic@fnmode{2}\@didactic@fnchanges\z@\fi
           \fi}}}}%
  \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnmode}{\@didactic@fntwo}{%
    \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnnow}{\@didactic@fnn}{%
      \def\@didactic@fnmode{1}\@didactic@fnchanges\z@}{}}{}%
  \if@filesw
    \immediate\write\@auxout{\string\@didactic@footcitestate
      {\@didactic@fnserial}{\@didactic@fnnow}{\@didactic@fnmode}%
      {\the\@didactic@fnchanges}}%
  \fi
  \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnmode}{\@didactic@fntwo}{%
    \PackageWarning{didactic}{The footnote for \thefield{entrykey}\space
      on page \zref@extractdefault{\@didactic@fnhere}{page}{??}\MessageBreak
      repeats one on the same page, since\MessageBreak
      reusing it didn't settle}%
    \@didactic@fnreusefalse}{}}
% \@didactic@fnnewnote{<text>}: a real footnote, with a label inside its text
% for the page where it is printed.
\def\@didactic@fnnewnote#1{%
  \edef\@didactic@fnnotelabel{didacticfnnote@\@didactic@fnserial}%
  \mkbibfootnote{#1\expandafter\zlabel\expandafter{\@didactic@fnnotelabel}}%
  \expandafter\xdef\csname @didactic@fncite@\@didactic@fnkey\endcsname
    {\the\c@footnote}%
  \expandafter\xdef\csname @didactic@fnlabel@\@didactic@fnkey\endcsname
    {\@didactic@fnnotelabel}%
  \expandafter\xdef\csname @didactic@fnmark@\@didactic@fnkey\endcsname
    {\@didactic@fnhere}%
}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% The invisible footnote is made by |\@didactic@fnghost|.
% It replaces the vertical list in |\@tempboxa| by an invisible one with the 
% same structure: every line becomes an empty box of the same size, and glue, 
% kerns and penalties stay.
% That way margin notes and page breaks treat it like the footnote.
% We take the list apart from the bottom with e-\TeX's |\lastnodetype|.
% If we meet anything else, like a rule or a whatsit, we can't take it out; 
% then the result is one empty box of the same size.
% The footnote must start in a list that isn't empty, as it does in the 
% margin, or \TeX\ leaves out the |\parskip| glue.
% That glue is 0\,pt there, but it is still a place where the list may break, 
% so to keep the list the same, the caller puts a |\kern\z@| first, and we 
% drop the kern at the top.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newbox\@didactic@ghostbox
\newbox\@didactic@ghostitem
\newif\if@didactic@ghostok
\def\@didactic@ghostput#1{%
  \global\setbox\@didactic@ghostbox\vbox{#1\unvbox\@didactic@ghostbox}}
\def\@didactic@ghostloop{%
  \let\@didactic@next\@didactic@ghostloop
  \ifcase\numexpr\lastnodetype+1\relax
    \let\@didactic@next\relax                                    % empty
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % char
  \or \@didactic@ghostcopy\hbox                                 % hlist
  \or \@didactic@ghostcopy\vbox                                 % vlist
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % rule
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % ins
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % mark
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % adjust
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % ligature
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % disc
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % whatsit
  \or \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax  % math
  \or \skip@\lastskip \unskip \@didactic@ghostput{\vskip\skip@}% glue
  \or \dimen@\lastkern \unkern                                 % kern,
      \ifnum\lastnodetype<\z@\else\@didactic@ghostput{\kern\dimen@}\fi % not ours
  \or \count@\lastpenalty \unpenalty
      \@didactic@ghostput{\penalty\count@}%                    penalty
  \else \global\@didactic@ghostokfalse \let\@didactic@next\relax
  \fi
  \@didactic@next}
% \@didactic@ghostcopy\hbox or \vbox: an empty box as large as the last box.
\def\@didactic@ghostcopy#1{%
  \setbox\@didactic@ghostitem\lastbox
  \dimen@\ht\@didactic@ghostitem \dimen@ii\dp\@didactic@ghostitem
  \dimen@i\wd\@didactic@ghostitem
  \setbox\@didactic@ghostitem#1{}%
  \ht\@didactic@ghostitem\dimen@ \dp\@didactic@ghostitem\dimen@ii
  \wd\@didactic@ghostitem\dimen@i
  \@didactic@ghostput{\box\@didactic@ghostitem}}
\def\@didactic@fnghost{%
  \global\setbox\@didactic@ghostbox\vbox{}%
  \global\@didactic@ghostoktrue
  \setbox\z@\vbox{\unvcopy\@tempboxa \@didactic@ghostloop}%
  \if@didactic@ghostok
    \setbox\@tempboxa\vbox{\unvbox\@didactic@ghostbox}%
  \else
    \setbox\z@\hbox{}%
    \ht\z@\ht\@tempboxa \dp\z@\dp\@tempboxa \wd\z@\wd\@tempboxa
    \setbox\@tempboxa\vbox{\box\z@}%
  \fi}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% A citation that reserves space and reuses a footnote uses 
% |\@didactic@fnreserve{<text>}|: the mark of the earlier footnote, padded to 
% the width its own mark would have, and an invisible footnote with the 
% structure of its own.
% We typeset its own footnote with the next number, as |\footnote| would, and 
% keep only its structure.
% The padding is a kern, which leaves the space factor after the mark as 
% |\footnotemark| sets it.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\def\@didactic@fnreserve#1{%
  \begingroup
    \advance\c@footnote\@ne
    \protected@edef\@thefnmark{\thefootnote}%
    \sbox\z@{\@makefnmark}\global\@didactic@fnmarkwd\wd\z@
    \c@footnote\csname @didactic@fncite@\@didactic@fnkey\endcsname\relax
    \protected@edef\@thefnmark{\thefootnote}%
    \sbox\z@{\@makefnmark}\global\advance\@didactic@fnmarkwd-\wd\z@
  \endgroup
  \footnotemark[\csname @didactic@fncite@\@didactic@fnkey\endcsname]%
  \kern\@didactic@fnmarkwd
  \begingroup
    \advance\c@footnote\@ne
    \let\@didactic@makefntext\@makefntext
    \long\def\@makefntext##1{%
      \setbox\@tempboxa\vbox{\kern\z@\@didactic@makefntext{##1}}%
      \@didactic@fnghost
      \unvbox\@tempboxa}%
    \mkbibfootnotetext{#1}%
  \endgroup
}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% Inside a footnote, \textsf{biblatex} prints the citation itself, and inside 
% a |minipage|, footnotes have their own counter; there we leave the citation 
% to |\mkbibfootnote|.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\def\@didactic@footnoteormark#1{%
  \if@didactic@fnreuseoff
    \mkbibfootnote{#1}%
  \else
    \iftoggle{blx@footnote}
      {\mkbibfootnote{#1}}
      {\ifdefstring{\@mpfn}{footnote}
         {\@didactic@footnoteormark@{#1}}
         {\mkbibfootnote{#1}}}%
  \fi}
\def\@didactic@footnoteormark@#1{%
  % reuse identity: entry key + prenote + postnote, detokenized for csname safety
  \protected@edef\@didactic@fnkey{%
    \thefield{entrykey}@\thefield{prenote}@\thefield{postnote}}%
  \edef\@didactic@fnkey{\detokenize\expandafter{\@didactic@fnkey}}%
  % serial: MD5 of the identity and the number among its citations
  \edef\@didactic@fnid{\pdf@mdfivesum{\@didactic@fnkey}}%
  \ifcsdef{@didactic@fnk@\@didactic@fnid}{}
    {\csgdef{@didactic@fnk@\@didactic@fnid}{0}}%
  \csxdef{@didactic@fnk@\@didactic@fnid}{%
    \the\numexpr\csuse{@didactic@fnk@\@didactic@fnid}+1\relax}%
  \edef\@didactic@fnserial{%
    \@didactic@fnid-\csuse{@didactic@fnk@\@didactic@fnid}}%
  \edef\@didactic@fnhere{didacticfn@\@didactic@fnserial}%
  \expandafter\zlabel\expandafter{\@didactic@fnhere}%
  \ifcsundef{@didactic@fncite@\@didactic@fnkey}%
    {\@didactic@fnnewnote{#1}}%
    {\edef\@didactic@fnprev{\csname @didactic@fnlabel@\@didactic@fnkey\endcsname}%
     \@didactic@fnpa=\@didactic@fnabspage{\@didactic@fnprev}\relax
     \@didactic@fnpm=\@didactic@fnabspage
       {\csname @didactic@fnmark@\@didactic@fnkey\endcsname}\relax
     \@didactic@fnpb=\@didactic@fnabspage{\@didactic@fnhere}\relax
     \@didactic@fngb=\@didactic@fnpb \@didactic@fntogroup\@didactic@fngb
     % reuse only when both pages are resolved (>0) and the citation shares a
     % group with the earlier footnote or with its resolved mark
     \@didactic@fnreusefalse
     \@didactic@fnga=\@didactic@fnpa \@didactic@fntogroup\@didactic@fnga
     \ifnum\@didactic@fnga=\@didactic@fngb \@didactic@fnreusetrue\fi
     \ifnum\@didactic@fnpm>\z@
       \@didactic@fnga=\@didactic@fnpm \@didactic@fntogroup\@didactic@fnga
       \ifnum\@didactic@fnga=\@didactic@fngb \@didactic@fnreusetrue\fi
     \fi
     \ifnum\@didactic@fnpa<\@ne \@didactic@fnreusefalse\fi
     \ifnum\@didactic@fnpb<\@ne \@didactic@fnreusefalse\fi
     \@didactic@fnhold
     \if@didactic@fnreuse
       % no space before the mark, as \mkbibfootnote does
       \unspace
       \ifdefequal{\@didactic@fnmode}{\@didactic@fnone}
         {\@didactic@fnreserve{#1}}
         {\footnotemark[\csname @didactic@fncite@\@didactic@fnkey\endcsname]}%
     \else
       \@didactic@fnnewnote{#1}%
     \fi}%
}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% \textsf{biblatex}'s own |\footcite| runs the whole citation inside the 
% footnote.
% Ours makes the footnote itself, or only prints the mark, and is back in the 
% text when \textsf{biblatex} records that the entry has been cited.
% Styles like |verbose| keep separate records for the text and the footnotes 
% (|citetracker=context|), and they ask the footnote's record whether to 
% print the citation in full or short; so with the citation recorded in the 
% text's, every footnote would be in full.
% Hence we end the citation by switching to the footnote context, which the 
% group of the citation ends again, so that it is recorded as a footnote 
% citation, as with \textsf{biblatex}'s |\footcite|.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\DeclareCiteCommand{\footcite}%
  [\unspace]
  {\relax}
  {\usebibmacro{citeindex}%
    \@didactic@footnoteormark
      {\usebibmacro{prenote}\usebibmacro{cite}\usebibmacro{postnote}}%
    \toggletrue{blx@footnote}}
  {\textsuperscript{,}}
  {\relax}
\DeclareMultiCiteCommand{\footcites}{\footcite}{\textsuperscript{,}}
\DeclareAutoCiteCommand{footnote}[f]{\footcite}{\footcites}
%    \end{macrocode}
% And that concludes the code for reusing footnotes for references.
%
%    \begin{macrocode}
}{}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% We also want |csquotes| to use |\autocite| for its integrated citation 
% commands (|\textcquote| and friends).
% However, |\autocite| only exists if |biblatex| is loaded, and |csquotes| 
% checks at |\begin{document}| that the command exists.
% So we set it at the end of the preamble, and only if both packages are 
% loaded by then.
% That way the order in which the packages are loaded doesn't matter either.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\AtEndPreamble{%
  \@ifpackageloaded{csquotes}{%
    \@ifpackageloaded{biblatex}{%
      \SetCiteCommand{\autocite}%
    }{}%
  }{}%
}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% \subsection{A side-caption environment for \texttt{beamer}}
%
% \begin{environment}{sidecaption}
% When using |memoir|, we can use the |sidecaption| environment to put the 
% caption in the margin.
% However, when using |beamer| we don't have this environment, so we need to 
% provide it with a suitable behaviour.
% It's suitable to just translate it to a normal caption, as usually used in 
% |beamer|.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\ProvideDocumentEnvironment{sidecaption}{omo}{%
  \relax
}{
  \IfValueTF{#1}
    {\caption[#1]{#2\IfValueT{#3}{\label{#3}}}}
    {\caption{#2\IfValueT{#3}{\label{#3}}}}
}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{environment}
%
% \begin{macro}{\flushscap}
% We also want to provide a command to flush a figure towards the caption, 
% for |sidecapalign=off|.
% With |memoir|, it's used inside the contents of the |sidecaption|, before 
% |memoir| has decided which margin the caption goes in, so we decide it 
% here the same way first (|\m@mscapcheckside|; |memoir| does it again at 
% the end, with the same result, since the float is on the same page).
% With |sidecapalign=caption| the check isn't needed: the contents are then 
% as wide as they need, and placed next to the caption anyway.
% There we skip it, since it isn't quite the same check as |memoir|'s: in a 
% two-column document, |memoir| recognises a |figure*| by |\hsize|, which 
% inside the contents is |\sidecapfloatwidth| instead.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\NewDocumentCommand{\flushscap}{O{\centering}}{%
  \@ifclassloaded{memoir}{%
    \IfStrEq{\@didactic@sidecapalign}{caption}{}{\m@mscapcheckside}%
    \ifscapmargleft%
      \flushleft%
    \else%
      \flushright%
    \fi%
  }{%
    #1%
  }%
}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{macro}
%
% \subsection{Margin floats in the notes and on the slides}
%
% \begin{environment}{marginfigure}
% \begin{environment}{margintable}
% We keep |memoir|'s names for the margin floats instead of adding our own.
% New names would leave |memoir|'s environments as they are, but then a
% deck would have two names for the same thing, and a source written for
% |memoir| would have to be changed to work on the slides.
% With |memoir|'s names, the notes, the slides and a plain |memoir| document
% read the same.
% The price is that we change |memoir|'s environments when |memoir| is
% customized, as the user documentation says.
%
% |memoir| builds both on its internal environment |mem@margin@float|,
% which takes the offset and the float type, sets the contents in a box as
% wide as the margin and gives that box to |\marginpar|
% (|\marginfloatmarginmacro|), which \textsf{marginfix} has redefined.
% It starts with |\FloatBlock|, which ends the paragraph and, when an
% ordinary float is waiting, starts a new page.
% We make |\FloatBlock| do nothing inside our environments, after asking the
% option |marginfloatorder| what to do about the order of the floats, and we
% make the offset 0\,pt by default instead of $-1.2$\,ex.
% The offset is the vertical space at the top of the box, after an empty
% |\hbox| that is aligned with the anchor.
% \textsf{marginfix} keeps the boxes in the margin from overlapping, so a
% box that starts right under a note is set |\marginparpush| below it; a
% negative offset then moves the contents back up into that space.
% With 0\,pt, the contents start at the top of their box.
% Since the environment no longer ends the paragraph, it may stand inside
% one, and there it should add no space to the text, as |\marginpar|
% doesn't.
% |\marginpar| uses LaTeX's |\@bsphack| and |\@esphack| for this, but its
% |\@esphack| runs inside the environment, where there are no spaces to
% ignore.
% So we do |\@bsphack| ourselves when the environment begins, and at the
% end, when there was a space before it, we ignore the spaces after the
% environment (|\ignorespacesafterend|).
% |\@bsphack| sets |\@savsk| locally, and |\marginpar|'s own |\@bsphack|
% happens in the group of |mem@margin@float|, so ours is intact at the end.
%
% In the notes, a frame that isn't there (|\begin{frame}<presentation>|) is
% typeset all the same, in a box that |beamer| throws away.
% Inside it, a |\marginpar| goes wrong in two ways: between paragraphs, where the
% box counts as inner mode, it fails with an undefined |\@marbox|, and in a
% paragraph, where it doesn't, it takes float boxes that never come back, so
% \LaTeX\ reports lost floats and other floats are lost in fact.
% So we don't give the box to |\marginpar| in such a frame, and we take the
% number back, since nothing in the notes will show it.
% The order of the floats doesn't matter there either, and |\FloatBlock| would
% end a page from inside the box.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@ifhiddenframe{%
  \@tempswafalse
  \@ifundefined{beamer@donoframe}{}{%
    \ifbeamer@inframe
      \ifx\beamer@howtotreatframe\beamer@donoframe\@tempswatrue\fi
    \fi}%
  \if@tempswa\expandafter\@firstoftwo\else\expandafter\@secondoftwo\fi}
\newcommand\@didactic@aftermarginfloat{}
\newcommand\@didactic@marginfloat[2]{%
  \@didactic@ifhiddenframe
    {\let\marginfloatmarginmacro\@gobble
     \edef\@didactic@aftermarginfloat{%
       \noexpand\setcounter{#2}{\the\value{#2}}}}%
    {\@didactic@marginfloatorder@check{#2}}%
  \@bsphack
  \let\FloatBlock\relax
  \begin{mem@margin@float}[#1]{#2}}
\newcommand\@didactic@endmarginfloat{%
  \end{mem@margin@float}%
  \@didactic@aftermarginfloat
  \relax\ifhmode
    \spacefactor\@savsf
    \ifdim\@savsk>\z@\ignorespacesafterend\fi
  \fi}
%    \end{macrocode}
% With |inorder| we simply do what |memoir| does, before |\@bsphack|, so that
% the environment starts between paragraphs and there is no space to save.
% With |beside| we do nothing here, and the comparison below reports the outcome.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@blockfloatorder[1]{\FloatBlock}
\IfStrEqCase{\@didactic@marginfloatorder}{%
  {beside}{\let\@didactic@marginfloatorder@check\@gobble}%
  {inorder}{\let\@didactic@marginfloatorder@check\@didactic@blockfloatorder}%
}[\PackageError{didactic}{%
    Unknown value `\@didactic@marginfloatorder'\MessageBreak
    for marginfloatorder}{Use beside or inorder.}%
  \let\@didactic@marginfloatorder@check\@gobble]
%    \end{macrocode}
% We only change |memoir|'s environments when we customize |memoir|, and
% only if they are built as we expect.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\if@didactic@memoir
\@ifundefined{mem@margin@float}{%
  \PackageWarningNoLine{didactic}{%
    Can't find memoir's margin floats; marginfigure\MessageBreak
    and margintable stay as memoir defines them}%
}{%
  \renewenvironment{marginfigure}[1][0pt]
    {\@didactic@marginfloat{#1}{figure}}
    {\@didactic@endmarginfloat}%
  \renewenvironment{margintable}[1][0pt]
    {\@didactic@marginfloat{#1}{table}}
    {\@didactic@endmarginfloat}%
}
\fi
%    \end{macrocode}
% Without |memoir|, there is no margin to put the float in, so the
% environments make an ordinary float: on the slides a |figure| or |table|
% in the frame, elsewhere one that floats.
% We centre the contents, since a margin float is usually narrower than the
% text.
% The optional argument, the offset, has no use there and is ignored.
% If the environments already exist, from |memoir| (with |nomemoir|) or
% another package, we leave them.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\ProvideDocumentEnvironment{marginfigure}{o}
  {\begin{figure}\centering}{\end{figure}}
\ProvideDocumentEnvironment{margintable}{o}
  {\begin{table}\centering}{\end{table}}
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% Then the order of the numbers.
% We can't tell from the state of \LaTeX\ when a float starts whether its number
% will come out of page order: a float earlier in the same paragraph isn't in
% any list yet, and \textsf{marginfix} can move a margin float later.
% So we look at the outcome: where each caption was shipped out.
%
% |\addcontentsline| is where we see a caption, since every way of making one
% comes through it with the name of the counter: the kernel, |memoir| with its
% side captions, \textsf{hyperref}, \textsf{float}'s own captions and
% \textsf{longtable}.
% |\@caption| doesn't do for the last two, and |\refstepcounter| doesn't do for
% \textsf{longtable} and has an optional argument with \textsf{cleveref}.
% Next to the entry for the list, in the same box, we write a line to the
% \texttt{.aux} with the counter, a running number for the order in which the
% numbers were given, the number as printed, and the input line, all expanded now,
% and the page, printed and absolute, expanded when the page is shipped out.
% The running number is what makes a counter that starts over in each chapter
% harmless, and the absolute page a page number that starts over.
% A frame that isn't there (|\begin{frame}<presentation>|) ends in a box that is
% thrown away, and this line with it.
%
% The line goes to the main \texttt{.aux} even in a file of |\include|.
% With |\includeonly|, the \texttt{.aux} of a chapter left out is what an
% earlier run wrote, and it is read too; its running numbers and pages would
% be compared with this run's and give false warnings.
% The main \texttt{.aux} holds this run and nothing else.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcount\@didactic@floatseq
\newcommand\@didactic@floatrec[6]{}
\newcommand\@didactic@recordfloat[2]{%
  \if@filesw
    \edef\@didactic@tmp{#1}\def\@didactic@tmpb{toc}%
    \ifx\@didactic@tmp\@didactic@tmpb\else
      \@ifundefined{c@#2}{}{%
        \global\advance\@didactic@floatseq\@ne
        \protected@write\@mainaux{}{%
          \string\@didactic@floatrec{#2}{\the\@didactic@floatseq}%
          {\csname the#2\endcsname}{\thepage}%
          {\noexpand\the\ReadonlyShipoutCounter}{\the\inputlineno}}%
      }%
    \fi
  \fi}
\newcommand\@didactic@contentsline[2]{%
  \@didactic@recordfloat{#1}{#2}%
  \@didactic@addcontentsline{#1}{#2}}
\newcommand\@didactic@recordfloats{%
  \if@filesw
    \immediate\write\@mainaux{%
      \string\providecommand\string\@didactic@floatrec[6]{}}%
  \fi
  \let\@didactic@addcontentsline\addcontentsline
  \let\addcontentsline\@didactic@contentsline}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \LaTeX\ reads the \texttt{.aux} at the start of the document, where the lines
% do nothing, and again at the end, after the last page, where they are compared.
% That reading is of what this run wrote, so it takes no extra run, and it
% changes nothing that \LaTeX\ compares to ask for one.
%
% The lines are in the order of the pages.
% For each counter we keep the highest running number of the page we're on and the
% highest one of the pages before it.
% A caption with a lower running number than that last one has been overtaken; a
% lower running number on the same page doesn't count.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@fo[2]{\csname @didactic@fo@#1@#2\endcsname}
\newcommand\@didactic@foset[3]{%
  \expandafter\gdef\csname @didactic@fo@#1@#2\endcsname{#3}}
\newcommand\@didactic@focopy[3]{%
  \expandafter\global\expandafter\let\csname @didactic@fo@#1@#2\expandafter
    \endcsname\csname @didactic@fo@#1@#3\endcsname}
\newcommand\@didactic@floatcheck[6]{%
  \@ifundefined{@didactic@fo@#1@abspage}{%
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{abspage}{#5}%
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{curseq}{0}%
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{prevseq}{0}%
  }{}%
  \ifnum#5>\@didactic@fo{#1}{abspage}\relax
    \ifnum\@didactic@fo{#1}{curseq}>\@didactic@fo{#1}{prevseq}\relax
      \@didactic@focopy{#1}{prevseq}{curseq}%
      \@didactic@focopy{#1}{prevnum}{curnum}%
      \@didactic@focopy{#1}{prevpage}{curpage}%
      \@didactic@focopy{#1}{prevline}{curline}%
    \fi
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{abspage}{#5}%
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{curseq}{0}%
  \fi
  \ifnum#2<\@didactic@fo{#1}{prevseq}\relax
    \PackageWarningNoLine{didactic}{%
      #1 #3 on page #4 comes after #1 \@didactic@fo{#1}{prevnum}
      on page \@didactic@fo{#1}{prevpage}:\MessageBreak
      the numbers are out of page order (captions at input\MessageBreak
      lines #6 and \@didactic@fo{#1}{prevline}).
      marginfloatorder=inorder or\MessageBreak
      \string\FloatBlock\space before the later number keeps the order}%
  \fi
  \ifnum#2>\@didactic@fo{#1}{curseq}\relax
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{curseq}{#2}%
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{curnum}{#3}%
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{curpage}{#4}%
    \@didactic@foset{#1}{curline}{#6}%
  \fi}
\@ifclassloaded{beamer}{}{%
  \AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\@didactic@recordfloats}%
  \AddToHook{enddocument/afterlastpage}{%
    \let\@didactic@floatrec\@didactic@floatcheck}%
}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{environment}
% \end{environment}
%
% \subsection{Frame titles in the notes}
%
% In the article mode, |beamer| typesets a frame title with the template
% |frametitle|, whose default is
% \begin{center}
% |\paragraph*{\insertframetitle}\ \par\noindent\emph{\insertframesubtitle}\par|
% \end{center}
% |\paragraph*| is a run-in heading: it typesets nothing at once, but
% leaves the title to be put in front of the next paragraph, when that
% starts.
% The |\ | starts that paragraph at once, with only the title in it, and
% the |\par| ends it, so the frame's text has to start a new paragraph.
% (The line with the subtitle, empty when there is none, is a third.)
% Our template keeps the run-in heading and drops the rest: the frame's
% first paragraph starts with the title.
% The subtitle goes into the heading, after an en dash, in italics and not
% in bold, so that it reads as part of the heading and not as the start of
% the text.
% A blank line after the title is still no new paragraph, since a |\par|
% between paragraphs does nothing, and a list or a block after the title
% puts the title on a line of its own, as it does after any run-in heading.
% |\frametitle| uses the template after the subtitle has been read, when
% one follows it (|\begin{frame}{Title}{Subtitle}| or
% |\frametitle{Title}\framesubtitle{Subtitle}|), so we can decide here
% whether there is one.
% |beamer| empties |\insertframesubtitle| at the start of each frame.
%
% We give |\paragraph*| the title itself and not |\insertframetitle|, since it
% is only typeset later, at the next paragraph: by then a second |\frametitle|
% in the frame (|\frametitle<2>|) may have changed |\insertframetitle|, which
% would lose the first title and set the second one twice.
% That is one step of expansion only: the title may hold anything that a frame
% title can hold, and a full expansion would fail on commands that aren't
% robust, which a frame title never had to be.
% So a macro in the title gets the meaning it has at that next paragraph.
%
% An empty title (|\begin{frame}{}|) makes no heading, and with a subtitle only
% the subtitle is the heading, without the dash.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@framehead{}
\protected\def\@didactic@frameheadused{%
  \global\let\@didactic@framehead\@empty}
\newcommand\@didactic@runinheading[1]{%
  \paragraph*{\@didactic@frameheadused #1}%
  \gdef\@didactic@framehead{#1}%
  \aftergroup\@didactic@endframetitle}
\newcommand\@didactic@frametitle{%
  \ifx\insertframesubtitle\@empty
    \ifx\insertframetitle\@empty\else
      \expandafter\@didactic@runinheading\expandafter{\insertframetitle}%
    \fi
  \else
    \edef\@didactic@heading{%
      \ifx\insertframetitle\@empty\else
        \unexpanded\expandafter{\insertframetitle}%
        \noexpand\space\noexpand\textendash\noexpand\space
      \fi
      \noexpand\textmd{\noexpand\emph{%
        \unexpanded\expandafter{\insertframesubtitle}}}}%
    \expandafter\@didactic@runinheading\expandafter{\@didactic@heading}%
  \fi}
%    \end{macrocode}
% The title waits for the next paragraph in |\everypar|, which is local to
% the frame's group.
% A frame with nothing after the title that starts a paragraph---only a
% margin note, say---would therefore lose the title when the frame ends, and
% leave \LaTeX\ believing a run-in heading is still waiting.
% So the heading empties |\@didactic@framehead| when it is typeset, and after the
% group in which |\frametitle| was used (|\aftergroup|), if it still isn't empty,
% we typeset it again as a paragraph of its own.
% This works for |\begin{frame}| and |\frame{...}| alike, where a hook at the end
% of the environment wouldn't be used for the second form.
% It comes after the box of a frame that isn't there
% (|\begin{frame}<presentation>|), so there we only forget the title.
% We don't look at |\if@noskipsec| to decide this, since that is also true for a
% |\paragraph| that the author wrote at the end of the frame, after the title has
% been set; that heading is lost as it would be at the end of any group.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@settitle{%
  \begingroup
    \leftskip\z@ \rightskip\@rightskip \parfillskip\@flushglue
    \leavevmode\par
  \endgroup}
\newcommand\@didactic@lateframetitle[1]{%
  \paragraph*{\@didactic@frameheadused #1}%
  \@didactic@settitle}
\newcommand\@didactic@endframetitle{%
  \ifx\@didactic@framehead\@empty\else
    \global\@noskipsecfalse
    \ifx\beamer@howtotreatframe\beamer@donoframe
      \@didactic@frameheadused
    \else
      \expandafter\@didactic@lateframetitle\expandafter
        {\@didactic@framehead}%
    \fi
  \fi}
%    \end{macrocode}
% A float can't wait for the title either: \LaTeX\ may set it right here (|[h]|,
% or |[H]| from the \textsf{float} package), so the figure would come above its
% title.
% So a float that starts while a title waits in a frame sets the title first.
% That also holds for a float that goes elsewhere, since we can't know where
% it will go.
%
% A box has the opposite problem: it starts the paragraph, so the title runs in
% with it, and a picture has the title at its baseline, as if it were a label.
% The boxes that a frame usually starts with therefore set the title first too,
% through their |before| hooks.
% |\tableofcontents| is among them, since the title would run into its heading.
% A box that isn't in the list stands beside the title; that can't be wrong in
% another way, and the user documentation says so.
%
% All of these only act while a title waits, in a frame, so they can't change
% text elsewhere: outside the frames, a run-in heading before a float or a box
% stays as it is.
% We set the title flush left and with an ordinary last line, whatever the frame
% has set up (|\centering| before the picture, say): the frame's text isn't
% typeset yet, so the title was never in that setup.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@titlefirst[1]{%
  \AddToHook{#1/before}{\@didactic@flushframetitle}}
\newcommand\@didactic@flushframetitle{%
  \ifbeamer@inframe
    \if@noskipsec\@didactic@settitle\fi
  \fi}
%    \end{macrocode}
% We use the template only in the article mode, unless the option
% |noarticle| is given.
% |beamerarticle| is often loaded after |didactic|, so we wait until it's
% loaded (or run at once if it already is).
% A template the user sets in the preamble after that still wins, because it
% comes later.
% One that the user set before |didactic|, after |beamerarticle|, wins because we
% only replace |beamerarticle|'s default: |\ifx| compares what is set to what
% setting the default makes.
%
% |\tableofcontents| doesn't get a hook, but we set the title in front of it at the
% beginning of the document, after the classes and packages have defined it:
% before the kernel of 2023-06, the hook on |beamerarticle|'s |\tableofcontents| fails
% with an illegal parameter number, and we ask for an older kernel than that.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\if@didactic@article
\newcommand\@didactic@articletitles{%
  \begingroup
    \setbeamertemplate{frametitle}[default]%
    \global\let\@didactic@defaulttitle\beamer@@tmpl@frametitle
  \endgroup
  \defbeamertemplate<article>{frametitle}{didactic}{\@didactic@frametitle}%
  \ifx\beamer@@tmpl@frametitle\@didactic@defaulttitle
    \setbeamertemplate{frametitle}[didactic]%
  \fi
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/figure}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/figure*}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/table}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/table*}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/tabular}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/tabular*}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/tabularx}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/minipage}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/tikzpicture}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{env/columns}%
  \@didactic@titlefirst{cmd/includegraphics}%
  \AtBeginDocument{%
    \let\@didactic@tableofcontents\tableofcontents
    \def\tableofcontents{%
      \@didactic@flushframetitle\@didactic@tableofcontents}}%
}
\@ifpackageloaded{beamerarticle}
  {\@didactic@articletitles}
  {\AddToHook{package/beamerarticle/after}{\@didactic@articletitles}}
\fi
%    \end{macrocode}
%
% \paragraph{Block titles.}
% The titles of |beamer|'s blocks (|block|, |alertblock| and 
% |exampleblock|) are on a line of their own in the article mode too: the 
% templates |block begin| and the like of \textsf{beamerbasearticle} set 
% the title, end the paragraph and start the block's text in a new one.
% We make them run-in headings as well, with the same space before as 
% |beamer| leaves, and in its fonts: bold, bold italic for an alerted block 
% and italic for an example block.
% We use |\@startsection| directly, as |\paragraph*| does, with level 4 and 
% the space of 1\,em after the title, so that a block that starts with a 
% list, a display or a listing gets the title on a line of its own, and so 
% that it counts as a heading for |keepafterheading|.
% The title goes in as it is now, not as |\insertblocktitle|: it waits for the 
% next paragraph, and if that is in a block inside this one, the name means the 
% title of that block by then.
% A block without a title gets none, and its text starts without an indent, 
% as in \textsf{beamerbasearticle}; a title that waits for a 
% paragraph when the block ends is set on a line of its own, as for the 
% frames.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\newcommand\@didactic@blocktitle[1]{%
  \ifx\insertblocktitle\@empty
    \par\vskip\medskipamount\noindent\ignorespaces
  \else
    \expandafter\@didactic@blockhead\expandafter{\insertblocktitle}{#1}%
  \fi}
\newcommand\@didactic@blockhead[2]{%
  \@startsection{paragraph}{4}{\z@}{\medskipamount}{-1em}%
    {\normalfont#2}*{#1}}
\newcommand\@didactic@endblock{%
  \if@noskipsec\leavevmode\par\fi
  \vskip\medskipamount}
\if@didactic@article
\AtEndOfPackageFile*{beamerarticle}{%
  \defbeamertemplate<article>{block begin}{didactic}%
    {\@didactic@blocktitle{\bfseries}}%
  \defbeamertemplate<article>{block end}{didactic}{\@didactic@endblock}%
  \defbeamertemplate<article>{block alerted begin}{didactic}%
    {\@didactic@blocktitle{\bfseries\itshape}}%
  \defbeamertemplate<article>{block alerted end}{didactic}%
    {\@didactic@endblock}%
  \defbeamertemplate<article>{block example begin}{didactic}%
    {\@didactic@blocktitle{\itshape}}%
  \defbeamertemplate<article>{block example end}{didactic}%
    {\@didactic@endblock}%
  \setbeamertemplate{block begin}[didactic]%
  \setbeamertemplate{block end}[didactic]%
  \setbeamertemplate{block alerted begin}[didactic]%
  \setbeamertemplate{block alerted end}[didactic]%
  \setbeamertemplate{block example begin}[didactic]%
  \setbeamertemplate{block example end}[didactic]%
}
\fi
%    \end{macrocode}
