%
% \begin{macro}{\FV@EndList}
% \begin{macro}{\@didactic@keep@check}
% What holds a listing to its lead-in can't always hold.
% What is kept with a listing can be taller than the five lines we left room 
% for (a large box in the paragraph before it), or a float can take the top 
% of the next page: the page is overfull.
% We can't take that back: the lines before the listing are on the page by 
% then, without a place to break before the listing.
% And something on its own between the lead-in and the listing gives a place to 
% break whatever comes next: a note or a float (|\addpenalty| in |\@specialoutput|), 
% space.
% If what comes doesn't fit, the listing starts the next page, and the lead-in stays
% behind.
%
% We can't foresee either, but we can see them when they have happened, from the page
% itself, with no estimate in between.
% \TeX\ looks at the page at the first place to break after the first line: the 
% first penalty that allows it, if the listing has one, else the penalty at the end 
% of the list, so we look there too.
% \begin{itemize}
% \item If the page is overfull, it has ended right here, for it had no earlier place 
%   to break, so the next page is still empty, which |\pagegoal| tells.
%   Nothing else leaves the page empty here.
% \item If \TeX\ ended the page before the listing, then the page now holds the 
%   listing up to here, and only that.
%   |\pagetotal| and |\pagedepth| hold at least a line more if anything comes before 
%   it.
%   We know the height, |\@didactic@keep@top|: what the first line and the rest make, 
%   with |\topskip| above the first line, and what came before the first line.
%   We allow half a line, for the depth that |\addpenalty| takes off.
%   A break within the lead-in leaves its last line on the page, so that doesn't 
%   count.
% \item If you asked for the break, or the listing has no lead-in, the page ended at a
%   negative penalty, which |\outputpenalty| tells: |\pagebreak|, |\goodbreak|,
%   |\smallbreak|, |\newpage| or the end of a list, also of another listing.
%   The start of a list that wraps the listing, such as |center|, gives a negative
%   penalty too, |\@beginparpenalty|, which nobody asked for.
%   So when the listing comes first in a list (|\if@newlist|), we note that value,
%   |\@didactic@keep@listpen|, and such a penalty counts as none.
%   Only if it is the document's, though: another one, by the key |beginpenalty| of
%   \textsf{enumitem} say, is yours, as it is for a list (|\@didactic@keep@listtie|),
%   so a page that ended at it, whatever its sign, ended where you asked
%   (|\@didactic@keep@yourpen|).
%   We note it once, for the outermost thing we set up, before that changes the value.
%   A break you ask for right before such a list ends the page at the list's penalty all
%   the same, for \TeX\ takes the last of breaks that cost the same, as all do on a
%   page that can't stretch.
%   So the commands for it set |\if@didactic@keep@asked|, until the next paragraph
%   starts, and then we don't note the value.
%   The others end the paragraph first, but |\pagebreak| in a paragraph is a
%   |\vadjust|: a break between the lines of the lead-in, not one before what it leads
%   into, unless nothing follows it in the paragraph.
%   We can tell that only at the end of the paragraph, so in a paragraph it notes that
%   it came (|\@didactic@keep@adjust|), and at the end we look at the last thing in the
%   paragraph (|\@didactic@keep@lastadjust|).
%   If that is a |\vadjust| and the last one was ours, the break is after the last
%   line, and the paragraph's end sets |\if@didactic@keep@asked|.
%   (\LaTeX\ ends the paragraph with |\nobreak| and |\parfillskip| anyway, so the
%   |\nobreak| and the space that |\@Esphack| adds after a float can go.)
%   A float or a margin note in a paragraph is a |\vadjust| too, and so is a
%   |\pagebreak| in it that doesn't end it; they come on the page after the line they
%   are in, before the paragraph has ended, so the check of a list can't see them
%   there.
%   A float ends in a place to break (|\addpenalty| in |\@specialoutput|); a margin
%   note doesn't, as \textsf{marginfix} sets |\outputpenalty| to 0 for it.
%   What came after the last line we see at the end of the paragraph
%   (|\@didactic@keep@afterline|): if the last thing on the page is glue or a penalty
%   that allows a break, it came there, as a paragraph ends in a line
%   (|\if@didactic@keep@parfloat|).
%   A footnote after the float hides that, as its insertion comes last, so the output
%   routine also notes where the page stood when it held a page for a float of the
%   paragraph (|\@didactic@keep@outrec|): if that is where the paragraph ended, on the
%   same page, the float was in its last line.
%   A float that \LaTeX\ sets right there (|h|) comes after that point, with
%   |\intextsep| above and below it, so then we count it in (|\@didactic@keep@outplaced|,
%   at the end of the output routine, when \LaTeX\ has put it on |\@midlist|).
%   What else ends the paragraph after a |\pagebreak|, a footnote, say, makes it count
%   as a break within the lead-in, and then what is parted warns.
%   A note, a float or glue gives a penalty of 0 or more, and then we warn, unless
%   we released the tie ourselves (|\if@didactic@keep@movable|): that has a warning
%   of its own, |\@didactic@keep@pp|.
%   If the page was empty already when the first line came, |\outputpenalty| may be from 
%   a note since then; so we keep the value from the last page that was shipped out.
%   The same if a page was shipped out since the listing started
%   (|\@didactic@keep@startship|): code side by side blocks the margin with a note
%   after its start, and if the page ended before that, the note sets
%   |\outputpenalty| on the next page, to a value that looks as if you asked.
% \item Right after the end of another list, also of a listing, a center or code side
%   by side, nothing leads into it: that is a place to break where \LaTeX\ means one,
%   whatever wraps the listing and whatever penalty the page ended at, a list's or
%   the note that ends code side by side, so nothing warns
%   (|\if@didactic@keep@pastlist|, noted with the list's penalty).
% \end{itemize}
%    \begin{macrocode}
\let\@didactic@FV@EndList\FV@EndList
\def\FV@EndList{%
  \@didactic@FV@EndList
  \if@didactic@keep@due
    \global\@didactic@keep@duefalse
    \@didactic@keep@check{A listing}%
  \fi}
\newcount\@didactic@keep@lastpen
\@didactic@keep@lastpen-\@M
\let\@didactic@keep@shipped\relax
\AddToHook{shipout/before}{%
  \if@fcolmade\else
    \global\@didactic@keep@lastpen\outputpenalty
    \xdef\@didactic@keep@shipped{\thepage}%
  \fi}
\def\@didactic@keep@fullpage{%
  \if@twocolumn
    \if@firstcolumn\@didactic@keep@shipped\else\thepage\fi
  \else
    \@didactic@keep@shipped
  \fi}
\def\@didactic@keep@check#1{%
  \if@didactic@keep@watch
    \ifdim\pagegoal=\maxdimen
      \PackageWarningNoLine{didactic}{%
        #1 doesn't fit on a page with what is kept\MessageBreak
        with it, so page \@didactic@keep@fullpage\space is overfull. Put a place to\MessageBreak
        break before it, such as \string\goodbreak, on input line
        \@didactic@keep@inputline\space of \@didactic@keep@inputfile}%
    \else
      \if@didactic@keep@pastlist\else
      \if@didactic@keepleadin\if@didactic@keep@movable
        \ifdim\dimexpr\pagetotal+\pagedepth\relax
            <\dimexpr\@didactic@keep@top+.5\baselineskip\relax
          \if@didactic@keep@emptystart
            \@tempcnta\@didactic@keep@lastpen
          \else
            \@tempcnta\outputpenalty
          \fi
          \ifnum\ReadonlyShipoutCounter>\@didactic@keep@startship\relax
            \@tempcnta\@didactic@keep@lastpen
          \fi
          \ifnum\@tempcnta=\@didactic@keep@listpen\@tempcnta\z@\fi
          \ifnum\@tempcnta=\@didactic@keep@yourpen\@tempcnta\m@ne\fi
          \ifnum\@tempcnta<\z@\else
            \PackageWarningNoLine{didactic}{%
              #1 starts page \thepage, apart from what leads\MessageBreak
              into it: a note, a float or space between them\MessageBreak
              gave a place to break, on input line
              \@didactic@keep@inputline\space of \@didactic@keep@inputfile}%
          \fi
        \fi
      \fi\fi\fi
    \fi
  \fi}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{macro}
% \end{macro}
%
% \begin{macro}{\@didactic@keep@pairstart}
% \begin{macro}{\@didactic@keep@pairmeasure}
% \begin{macro}{\@didactic@keep@pairend}
% A pair is one box in a paragraph of its own.
% It gets the same checks: the first thing on the page, and the only one, if the box 
% is, when \TeX\ has looked at the end of the list.
% The tabular is the last box of its line at its end, so we can take it, measure it 
% and put it back.
% Listings in its cells have been through |\@didactic@keep@flush| by then, in a box, 
% so only now we set what the check looks at.
% A list that starts right after the |\item| of another list has no penalty at its end,
% only the outer list has, so \TeX\ hasn't looked at the page yet if that is too full.
% Then we make it look, where it would break anyway.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\def\@didactic@keep@pairstart{%
  \@didactic@keep@notelist
  \@didactic@keep@atstart
  \@didactic@keep@noteempty
  \ifinner\else
    \if@didactic@keep@cols
      \PackageWarningNoLine{didactic}{%
        Code side by side in multicols isn't tied to what\MessageBreak
        leads into it: we can't know the height of its\MessageBreak
        column, \@didactic@keep@place}%
    \fi
  \fi}
\def\@didactic@keep@pairmeasure{%
  \setbox\@tempboxa\lastbox
  \@tempdima\dimexpr\topskip-\ht\@tempboxa\relax
  \ifdim\@tempdima<\z@\@tempdima\z@\fi
  \global\@didactic@keep@top
    \dimexpr\@tempdima+\ht\@tempboxa+\dp\@tempboxa\relax
  \box\@tempboxa}
\def\@didactic@keep@pairend{%
  \global\@didactic@keep@watchtrue
  \ifinner\global\@didactic@keep@watchfalse\fi
  \ifinner\else
    \ifdim\pagetotal>\pagegoal
      \penalty\@endparpenalty
    \fi
  \fi
  \@didactic@keep@check{Code side by side}}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{macro}
% \end{macro}
% \end{macro}
%
% \begin{macro}{\FV@ListVSpace}
% Before the listing, \textsf{fancyvrb} adds |\@beginparpenalty|, as a 
% \LaTeX\ list does, which by default invites a page break there.
% With |keepleadin| we forbid it, so the paragraph before stays with the 
% listing, unless moving them would leave too much empty space 
% (|\@didactic@keep@tie|).
% Breaks within that paragraph are still allowed.
% In a chunk of \textsf{noweb} the line before is its header, which never parts
% from the code; the chunk has settled the tie before its header.
%
% A listing right under a heading has nothing to be released from, and a heading
% is never parted from what follows it (|\if@didactic@keep@direct|).
% But the limit holds here too: past |keepmaxgap| the heading stays and the listing
% breaks, with a warning, by the rule of |keepends|, so also one of at most
% |keeplines| lines.
% If that doesn't fit, or the listing has too few lines, all moves as \LaTeX\ moves
% it, and the pages say so at the end of the run.
%    \begin{macrocode}
\let\@didactic@FV@ListVSpace\FV@ListVSpace
\def\FV@ListVSpace{%
  \@didactic@keep@notelist
  \@didactic@keep@atstart
  \if@didactic@keep@inchunk
    \if@didactic@keepleadin
      \@beginparpenalty\@M
    \fi
  \else
    \@didactic@keep@directfalse
    \@didactic@keep@tie
    \if@didactic@keepleadin
      \if@didactic@keep@movable
        \@beginparpenalty\@M
      \fi
    \fi
    \@didactic@keep@underheading
    \if@didactic@keep@movable\else\if@didactic@keep@direct\else
      \ifinner\else\@didactic@keep@relstart\fi
    \fi\fi
  \fi
  \@didactic@FV@ListVSpace}
\def\@didactic@keep@underheading{%
  \ifinner\else
    \ifnum\@didactic@keep@headstate=\@ne
      \@didactic@keep@directtrue
      \@beginparpenalty\@M
    \fi
  \fi}
%    \end{macrocode}
% \end{macro}
%
% That keeps only the last line of the paragraph with the listing: the
% paragraph is on the page before the listing starts, and the breaks
% within it are settled.
% So a last line can stand alone above a listing, with the rest of its paragraph
% on the page before, and the penalties of the paragraph are as the class has them:
% \cref{PageBreakDecisions} says why.
% They wouldn't always prevent it anyway.
% \TeX's cost of a break is the badness of the page plus the penalty, but a page
% that falls short with too little stretch to fill it has the highest badness,
% and from then on every break costs the same, whatever the penalty below
% 10\,000, and \TeX\ takes the last one.
% It is the stretch of the page that decides this: \cs{flushbottom} and
% \cs{raggedbottom} break in the same places.
% The first paragraph after a heading is another matter: \LaTeX's
% |\@afterheading| forbids the break after its first line.
%
% \begin{macro}{\@didactic@keep@room}
% \begin{macro}{\@startsection}
% \begin{macro}{\@thm}
% A heading should have |keepafterheading| lines after it on its page.
% Three things see to that: a chain from the heading through the first lines 
% of text, which \TeX\ can't break (below); a place to break before the 
% heading, for what a chain can't hold, such as a list after a short paragraph 
% (here); and a look at the outcome, which warns when neither did it (below).
%
% Before a heading we can only estimate, from the room left on the page, like 
% \textsf{needspace} does: |\@didactic@keep@room|\{\meta{height}\} ends the
% page here if less than \meta{height} plus a line of the text are left.
% One line, for we don't know yet what comes: a list right under a heading has what
% \LaTeX\ gives it.
% The other lines of |keepafterheading| are for the chain, which moves the heading
% with \LaTeX's own place to break.
% We estimate as \TeX\ will decide: a break fits if the page less all that it can 
% shrink fits, so the room counts |\pageshrink|, which includes what 
% \textsf{memoir}'s |\sloppybottom| gives to |\topskip|, and \meta{height} is 
% what the heading's spaces can shrink to.
% But with a |keepmaxgap| we break only if no more than that many lines are 
% left, and those are the natural lines, without the shrinking: the limit on 
% empty space wins over the lines after a heading.
% Not after another heading (|\if@nobreak|), which has asked already, nor on 
% an empty page, nor inside a box.
% Nor when the text so far is already more than the page holds: then \TeX\ 
% has yet to break the page somewhere before, the rest goes to the next 
% page with the heading, and a break here would leave that rest alone on a 
% page.
% The break comes with a |\vfil|, as in |\newpage|: under |\flushbottom| the 
% space stays at the foot of the page rather than going to the gaps between 
% paragraphs, with an |Underfull \vbox|.
%
% The sectional headings, |\section| to |\subparagraph|, start with 
% |\@startsection|\{\meta{name}\}\{\meta{level}\}\{\meta{indent}\}\{\meta{beforeskip}\}
% \{\meta{afterskip}\}\{\meta{style}\}; |\chapter| doesn't.
% A heading on a line of its own (a positive \meta{afterskip}) needs that 
% line and the space after it; a run-in heading only the space before it, since 
% it starts the first line.
% We count that line as a line of the text: \meta{style} can do anything, and we
% don't run it to learn its font, since that would run all it does twice.
% From the line before it, that line takes the |\baselineskip| less |\prevdepth|,
% plus |\pagedepth|, which aren't the same when glue has come since that line;
% after a rule, or anything else that sets |\prevdepth| to $-1000$\,pt, there is
% no interline glue, and the line takes its height, which we count as four fifths
% of that.
% A heading in a larger font, or of more than one line, is more than we count, and
% the chain sees to that.
% The estimate may be low: the chain is exact where it holds, and a page ended too
% early can't be taken back; where the chain doesn't hold, the outcome warns.
%
% A heading is never parted from what follows it, which takes more than that for
% a run-in heading.
% Its title is set with the next paragraph, but a list, a block or another heading
% starts by setting the title on a line of its own, and then offers a place to
% break, |\@beginparpenalty| or |\@secpenalty|: the title would end the page alone.
% So when a title is waiting, we forbid that place.
% All that starts with |\@trivlist| comes through there, and we set the penalty in
% it, in the group of its environment; \textsf{fancyvrb} has its own start,
% |\FV@LeaveVMode|.
% Before another heading we set the title ourselves, with the space that the
% heading would have had, and tell \LaTeX\ that we are after a heading,
% |\@didactic@keep@forcetitle|.
% That line holds no line of the text, |\if@didactic@keep@titleonly|.
%
% Item lists are for |keepleadin|: |itemize|, |enumerate|, |description| and what
% \textsf{enumitem} calls lists start with a mark, |\if@didactic@keep@itemlist|, and
% |\@didactic@keep@listtie|, right after |\@trivlist| has ended the paragraph before,
% forbids the place to break at the start of the list, if moving what leads into it
% stays within |keepmaxgap|, as for a listing.
% Past the limit the bonus of $-51$ stays only if the first line of the first item
% can't fit, reckoned as \TeX\ does, with a line of the text.
% If it can, \TeX\ would still take the bonus on a page that stretches, and the warning
% would have no reason, so we make that break cost more than the one after that line
% can: its club and interline penalties, and 100 for the shrinking it may need.
% (The club penalty of \LaTeX, |\@clubpenalty|: |\clubpenalty| is 10000 here under a
% heading, until the next paragraph.)
% So the first item stays on the page if \TeX\ can have it there.
% We don't forbid the break: what follows may yet not fit, a first line taller than a
% line of the text, or the lead-in of a listing in the item, and then the list starts
% the next page, with the warning that says so, and no page is overfull.
% \TeX\ may then also find a break above the lead-in better, on a page that stretches.
% That takes the lead-in along, past the limit, and has a warning of its own
% (|\@didactic@keep@relmove|), which can only say what it can leave empty, since it
% doesn't know how many lines went.
% Under a heading it is about the last lines of the lead-in, at most two, so it is
% written only if they could leave too much, and if the heading went as well, the
% heading's warning says it and this one keeps quiet (|\@didactic@keep@lastmoved|).
% Only that penalty changes: between the items \LaTeX\ goes on as ever.
% What comes between the lead-in and the list, space, a note or a float, is a place
% to break that this penalty can't take away, as for a listing, and the list then
% goes to the next page without its lead-in, so we write the pages down here too, and
% that warns, unless you asked for the break.
% Right after the end of another list there is nothing to tie it to: that is a place
% to break where \LaTeX\ means one, and nothing warns, as for a listing there.
% (|\if@endpe| can't tell, as |\begin| clears it before we get here.)
% Only a list that ends on the page counts, not one in a box (|\ifinner|): a list in
% a note, a footnote, a minipage or a float in the lead-in ends in a list of its own,
% and the list after the lead-in is still tied to it.
% And only while nothing that prints comes after it: a box on the page, from
% |\centerline| or |\hbox|, starts no paragraph, but it can lead into what follows, so
% when a list or a listing starts and the last thing on the page is a box, the end
% of the list is behind us (|\@didactic@keep@boxcheck|), and the box is the end of
% what leads into it, as the end of a paragraph would be (|\@didactic@keep@boxlead|).
% After the end of a list itself the last thing is its glue, and a note or a float
% leaves a penalty or nothing.
% Where that page is written matters: after such a place it would be on the next page
% too, and nothing would seem parted.
% So |\@didactic@keep@ifbreakless| looks whether anything came after the lead-in's last
% line: glue or a penalty that \TeX\ still has, what it has already put on the page, by
% its height, or a note or a float, by |\outputpenalty|, or by
% |\if@didactic@keep@parfloat| if it ended the lead-in's paragraph, for then it was on
% the page before the paragraph ended.
% A rule right after the lead-in's last line is no place to break, as nothing comes
% between them, so if the last thing on the page is a rule (|\lastnodetype| 3, which
% looks at the page when \TeX\ has put everything there) we don't count its height
% (|\@didactic@keep@pendrule|), nor that of glue after |\nobreak| before it.
% A rule hides what came before it, though, so glue before it we see otherwise: we let
% \TeX\ put everything on the page first, with a |\par| in a group, which builds the
% page and changes nothing else; glue that can stretch or shrink changes the page's
% stretch and shrink from what they were at the end of the lead-in
% (|\@didactic@keep@pendstretch|), and |\vspace| or |\addvspace| after the lead-in notes
% that it came (|\if@didactic@keep@vspaced|), unless as the end of a list.
% Right after |\nobreak| the glue is no place to break, and nothing that it is followed
% by can be one but more glue of the primitives, so then it notes that instead
% (|\if@didactic@keep@nbglue|), and neither the glue at the end nor the page's height
% or stretch counts.
% What prints nothing and isn't glue, a |\label|, an entry for the contents, a colour,
% a mark, an insertion or |\nobreak|, hides what came before it, a rule say, so when it
% is the last thing on the page we allow the height of a thin rule, 2\,pt
% (|\@didactic@keep@hidden|); a line of text or glue before it is more than that.
% Glue of the primitive |\vskip| that neither stretches nor shrinks, before a rule, we
% still can't see.
% If so, we take the page from the tie instead.
