% A table set with \textsf{longtable} decides for itself where it starts: 
% if its head and first row don't fit on the page, it ends the page before 
% it, which can leave a heading and the sentence that leads into the table 
% at the foot of the page, whatever we estimated.
% That we learn only when the pages are shipped out, so we remember it for 
% the next run, as \textsf{marginfix} does for notes that don't fit.
%
% Each heading gets a key: a hash of its file with its path, its line, and how 
% many headings have started on that line so far.
% So two files of the same name in different directories don't collide, nor 
% do two readings of one file or several headings made by one macro, and the 
% key can be written to the |.aux| file and read back whatever the path holds.
% A heading right after another (|\if@nobreak|, or a run-in title still 
% waiting) has no key of its own: they stand or fall together, so all that 
% we learn is about the first of them, and a break can only go before that.
% \cs{@didactic@keep@headroom} is the room left on the page when the first of 
% them started, $-1$\,sp when it starts the page, which moving can't help.
%
% A heading writes its page to the |.aux| file (|\@didactic@keep@hpage|), at 
% shipout like |\label|: on a line of its own when it's done (|\@afterheading|), 
% run in when its first paragraph is (see below).
% |\@afterheading| is also what |\chapter| and others use, which aren't ours: 
% they end what we knew about the heading before them, so that a table after a 
% chapter's heading isn't taken for one after the last section.
%
% A table owes the heading before it lines when they are still due at its 
% start (the state, below).
% It writes the page of its first row, the room when the heading started and 
% whether that is over |keepmaxgap| (|\@didactic@keep@tp|).
% If the table turned out to start on a later page than the heading, and moving 
% is within the limit, the next run breaks before the heading.
% If it is over the limit, the limit wins, and we warn: the file and line of 
% the heading are in \cs{@didactic@hn@}\meta{key}, in memory, and we say it when 
% the |.aux| file is read at the end of the run, so once and about this run.
%
% A heading with more room than the limit can still end up on the next page: 
% \LaTeX\ moves it when what follows can't part from it, such as a tall box or the 
% first two lines of a paragraph.
% No estimate can say so; the pages can.
% So such a heading writes the page it ended up on, and the page where it started 
% (|\@didactic@keep@hm|), and if they differ we warn.
% The warning says what the pages did, in every such case, with no estimate behind 
% it.
%
% The room goes with the break into the |.aux| (|\@didactic@keep@hb|): the table 
% doesn't fit in that much room.
% The next run breaks only if the room is no more than that, and the option 
% and the limit allow it, and only then writes the break again.
% With more room, or with the option turned off, there is no break and no 
% record of one any more, whatever the old |.aux| says, and if the table still 
% doesn't fit we learn a larger room, so that it settles.
% A heading at the top of a page, or on a page already over full, doesn't 
% break.
%    \begin{macrocode}
