% The lines after a heading we count with the hooks of the paragraphs, and 
% keep together with e-\TeX's |\clubpenalties|: 10\,000 after each of the first 
% lines that are due but the last of them, and 0 after the rest.
% |\@didactic@keep@headstate| says where we are:
% 0 no lines are due;
% 1 a heading is done and waits for its first paragraph;
% 2 we are in a paragraph that counts;
% 3 between two such paragraphs, with lines still due;
% 4 a run-in title that was waiting takes the next paragraph for itself, and 
% then it is 1;
% 5 the lines are done, but the paragraph that did it may have all its lines 
% go along with a listing after it, and so the heading.
%
% |\clubpenalties| we set globally at the start of a paragraph and clear at its 
% end, so that nothing outlives the paragraph whatever groups there are.
% Paragraphs in boxes don't count: a note, a float, a cell of a table, the 
% head of a page in the output routine.
% They all come through |\@arrayparboxrestore|, or the output routine, or start 
% directly in a |\vbox|, where we set |\if@didactic@keep@inbox|, locally.
% They get no |\clubpenalties| from around them either.
% A display in the first paragraph is lines enough, and what follows it in the
% same paragraph would count its lines from the start: the penalties end there.
%
% At the start of the first paragraph the heading is on the page, so we can 
% measure, in vertical mode (|para/before|), again counting what the page can 
% shrink.
% The |\parskip| above the paragraph is on the page by then, if it gets one: \TeX\
% adds it before |\everypar|, where the hook runs, and after a heading of
% \textsf{memoir} there is none.
% The depth of the line before, the heading's, is on the page too, but the glue
% above the first line takes it back, as |\baselineskip| is from baseline to
% baseline: so each line takes a line of the text, and the first one that less
% |\prevdepth|, which we add to the room.
% If the lines don't fit, the chain will move the heading to the next page, 
% and if that is over |keepmaxgap| the limit wins: we ask for as many lines as 
% fit, and warn.
% A chapter that doesn't start a page, under \textsf{memoir}'s |article|, asks no
% lines of its own (|\if@didactic@keep@chaphead|, which every heading clears): \LaTeX\
% keeps the first two lines of its paragraph with it, and we only see to it that the
% paragraph starts on its page.
% If the first paragraph has fewer lines than that, the next one owes the rest, and
% it has a |\parskip| of its own, so at its start we measure again
% (|\@didactic@keep@remeasure|): if the rest doesn't fit we ask for what does, and
% warn, so that no more lines are owed than fit and no fewer stay than \LaTeX\
% would keep.
% Only if two lines or more are owed: one owed line is no club penalty, so it holds
% nothing, and if it doesn't fit, the paragraph goes to the next page and the pages
% say so at the end of the run, as before.
% That warning waits until the lines are done (|\@didactic@keep@short|), at the start
% of the next paragraph that owes none: if you end the page before that, with
% |\newpage|, nothing is due, and it says nothing.
