% \begin{macro}{\nwbegincode}
% \begin{macro}{\@begincode}
% A code chunk of \textsf{noweb} is a list too, with a header line, 
% \meta{chunk name}${}\equiv{}$, before the code.
% \textsf{noweb} puts |\@highpenalty| before the chunk, which doesn't keep 
% the chunk from being parted from its lead-in, and |\nwcodepenalty| after 
% each line, the header line too.
% With |keepleadin| we forbid both breaks: we make the penalty before the 
% chunk 10\,000, and the penalty after the first line of the chunk, the 
% header, |\@didactic@nwfirstpenalty|, which is 10\,000 in a chunk until 
% the header has used it, and |\nwcodepenalty| otherwise.
% When the chunk's code is set by |minted|, the breaks in the code itself
% are ours, as for every listing; when \textsf{noweb} sets the code, its
% breaks stay as they were.
% The tie to the lead-in holds only within |keepmaxgap| (|\@didactic@keep@tie|), 
% and it has to be settled before the header, which is on the page before the 
% code is known.
% So the penalty is set right after |\trivlist| has ended the paragraph before, 
% |\@didactic@nwtie|, and the code knows it is in a chunk, 
% |\if@didactic@keep@inchunk|.
% If the tie is released and the chunk then goes to the next page, we warn.
% (\TeX\ can still take the lead-in along, if a break above it costs less; the
% warning doesn't say otherwise.)
% \textsf{noweb} also invites a page break at the start of the text after 
% every chunk (|\nwdocspar|): a |\filbreak|, or with the option 
% |breakcode| a penalty of $-200$ with 0.2\,in of stretch, which in the 
% notes ends pages right after a chunk with several lines empty.
% With a |keepmaxgap| we leave the breaks to the rules above and drop it, also
% when |\noweboptions| comes later, as it does in the text with |noweave -delay|.
% The |\filbreak| that \textsf{noweb} has at the end of a chunk without 
% |breakcode| we can't drop as well as honour what the writer may want of it, and 
% it ends a page wherever the next chunk doesn't fit whole, so |keepmaxgap| can't 
% bound what that leaves empty.
% We say so once, at the first chunk, since |\noweboptions| can come as late as 
% the first text of the document.
%
% A chunk that \textsf{noweb} sets itself gets the check of |\@didactic@keep@check|
% too, and that works differently, since we hold no box with its lines.
% Every line is a paragraph of its own, ended by the |\par| that we patch, so we can
% look at the page around the header line and the first line of code: the first place
% to break comes after that first line, its |\nwcodepenalty|.
% |\@didactic@keep@nwa|, |nwb| and |nwc| are the height of the page before the header,
% after it, and after the first line, all before \TeX\ could end the page there.
% If the page then starts with the header, it holds at most |\topskip| and the depth
% of the header more than the step from |nwb| to |nwc|.
% If anything comes before the header, it holds at least all of the step from |nwa|
% to |nwb| more, which is the header and the space above it.
% So half of that step tells them apart, whatever the font, unless the header is
% twice as tall as |\topskip|.
% The page can't end within that first line: \textsf{noweb} makes the spaces in code
% unbreakable, so a line doesn't wrap.
% A chunk woven for |minted| has no such lines, and gets the check of a listing.
% \textsf{noweb} is usually loaded after us, so we patch it at the beginning 
% of the document, if it's loaded.
% |\patchcmd| reads the patched macro anew with the category codes of the 
% moment, and there |@| usually isn't a letter any more, so we make it one 
% for a while.
%    \begin{macrocode}
